/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/aws-security.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("aws-security", [["scs1",0,1,"An investigation needs to show which IAM principal deleted objects from a sensitive S3 bucket last week. The account has a trail with default settings. What should the security engineer have turned on to capture this?",["Management events in CloudTrail Event history","CloudTrail Insights events on the trail","S3 data events on the CloudTrail trail","Log file integrity validation on the trail"],2,"Object-level operations such as DeleteObject and GetObject are data events, which trails do not log unless you turn them on. Management events only cover bucket-level actions such as DeleteBucket, Insights detects unusual API rates, and integrity validation only proves logs were not altered.","Domain 1: CloudTrail data events",["Management events cover control-plane calls such as DeleteBucket or PutBucketPolicy, not object-level deletes inside a bucket.","Insights events flag unusual rates of API calls; they do not record each object deletion or who made it.",null,"Integrity validation proves log files were not altered, but it cannot add events that were never logged."],2],["scs2",0,1,"An auditor asks for proof that CloudTrail log files in S3 were not modified or deleted after delivery. What should the engineer use?",["Log file integrity validation and its digest files","S3 server access logging on the log bucket","An AWS Config rule for the trail's settings","SSE-KMS encryption of the delivered log files with a new key"],0,"Integrity validation delivers hourly digest files with hashes of each log file, signed by CloudTrail, and `validate-logs` checks them. Encryption protects confidentiality, not integrity proof, and access logs or Config only show who touched the bucket or how the trail is configured.","Domain 1: CloudTrail integrity validation",[null,"Server access logs show who accessed the log bucket, but they cannot prove the contents of each log file are unchanged.","A Config rule shows how the trail is configured, not whether individual log files were modified after delivery.","Encryption with a new key protects confidentiality; anyone allowed to write could still replace or delete a file."],1],["scs3",0,1,"A company has 60 accounts in AWS Organizations. It needs one trail that covers every account, including new ones, and that administrators in member accounts cannot stop or delete. What should it create?",["A separate trail in each account deployed by a CloudFormation StackSet","An EventBridge rule in each account that restarts logging","An AWS Config rule that checks for cloudtrail-enabled","An organization trail from the management or delegated admin account"],3,"An organization trail logs every member account automatically, and member accounts can see it but cannot modify or delete it. StackSet trails live in each account where local administrators could change them, and EventBridge or Config only react after logging has stopped.","Domain 1: organization trails",["Trails created in each account belong to that account, so its local administrators could still stop or delete them.","Restarting logging after the fact still leaves a gap, and administrators could delete the rule itself.","A Config rule only reports that CloudTrail is off; it does not prevent anyone from turning it off.",null],2],["scs4",0,1,"A team wants Amazon GuardDuty to detect an EC2 instance talking to known command-and-control IP addresses. VPC Flow Logs are not enabled in the account. What must the team do?",["Enable VPC Flow Logs to CloudWatch Logs in every VPC before GuardDuty starts","Nothing extra, because GuardDuty reads an independent flow log stream","Turn on GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring for every instance","Install the CloudWatch agent on every EC2 instance"],1,"GuardDuty's foundational data sources, including VPC Flow Logs, DNS logs and CloudTrail management events, are pulled from independent streams, so you do not need to enable or store the logs yourself. Runtime Monitoring adds OS-level visibility but is not needed for network-based findings, and the CloudWatch agent is unrelated.","Domain 1: GuardDuty data sources",["GuardDuty does not need your own flow logs; it reads an independent copy, so this step adds cost without being required.",null,"Runtime Monitoring adds process-level visibility on hosts, but network findings already work from foundational data sources.","The CloudWatch agent collects OS metrics and logs; GuardDuty does not use it for network threat detection."],2],["scs5",0,1,"A security team wants GuardDuty turned on in all 80 accounts of its organization, including accounts created later, and managed from the security tooling account. What should it do?",["Enable GuardDuty in each account and send email invitations from the security tooling account","Create an SCP that denies guardduty:DisableDetector in every OU","Designate the security account as GuardDuty delegated administrator and turn on auto-enable","Deploy a Lambda function that enables GuardDuty whenever an account is created"],2,"With Organizations, the management account designates a delegated administrator, which can auto-enable GuardDuty for existing and new member accounts. Invitations are the older, manual method for accounts outside an organization, an SCP stops disabling but does not enable anything, and a custom Lambda is unnecessary work.","Domain 1: GuardDuty delegated administrator",["Invitations are the manual method for accounts outside an organization, and they do not auto-enable new accounts.","An SCP can stop people disabling GuardDuty, but it cannot turn GuardDuty on in any account.",null,"Custom Lambda code duplicates a built-in feature and adds maintenance, failure points and permissions to manage."],2],["scs6",0,1,"Findings from AWS Security Hub are spread over five Regions. Analysts want to see and manage all of them from one Region. What should be configured?",["Cross-Region aggregation with a chosen home Region","A Security Hub custom action in each Region","An S3 bucket that receives findings from Firehose","A CloudWatch cross-account dashboard for each Region"],0,"Security Hub cross-Region aggregation replicates findings and updates from linked Regions into an aggregation Region, where they can be viewed and updated. Custom actions only send selected findings to EventBridge, and exporting to S3 or dashboards does not give a working findings view.","Domain 1: Security Hub aggregation",[null,"Custom actions send chosen findings to EventBridge for automation; they do not combine Regions into one view.","Exporting findings to S3 gives raw data for analytics, not a working findings view where analysts can update workflow status.","A dashboard can chart metrics but cannot list, triage and update Security Hub findings across Regions."],1],["scs7",0,1,"The security team needs a record of every DNS name that EC2 instances in a VPC resolve, so it can hunt for lookups of suspicious domains. Which log source provides this?",["VPC Flow Logs with the default format","Elastic Load Balancing access logs","CloudTrail management events","Route 53 Resolver query logging"],3,"Resolver query logging records the DNS queries made from resources in a VPC, including the queried name and response. Flow logs record IP-level connection metadata, not names, ELB logs record HTTP requests to the load balancer, and CloudTrail records API calls.","Domain 1: log sources",["Flow logs record IP addresses, ports and bytes, not the domain names that instances looked up.","ELB access logs record HTTP requests reaching the load balancer, not outbound DNS lookups from instances.","CloudTrail records AWS API calls, not the DNS queries that workloads make inside the VPC.",null],1],["scs8",0,1,"The CISO wants an email within minutes whenever the account root user signs in to the console. What is the simplest design?",["A daily Athena query over CloudTrail logs in S3 that emails a summary report","An EventBridge rule on the root sign-in event that targets an SNS topic","An AWS Config rule that checks root MFA status","A GuardDuty suppression rule for root activity"],1,"CloudTrail sends the console sign-in event to EventBridge, where a rule matching a Root userIdentity type can notify an SNS topic in near real time. A daily query is too slow, the Config rule only checks MFA configuration, and a suppression rule would hide findings rather than alert.","Domain 1: alerting with EventBridge",["A daily report arrives many hours after the sign-in, not within minutes as the CISO asked.",null,"This rule checks whether root has MFA; it does not detect or report each root sign-in.","Suppression rules hide or archive GuardDuty findings, which is the opposite of alerting on activity."],1],["scs9",0,1,"CloudTrail already delivers to a CloudWatch Logs log group. The team wants an alarm when more than 10 unauthorized API calls occur in five minutes. What should it create?",["A GuardDuty threat list containing the IP addresses that made the unauthorized calls","An S3 event notification on the trail bucket","A metric filter for AccessDenied and UnauthorizedOperation errors, with an alarm","A Security Hub insight grouped by the error code field"],2,"A metric filter turns matching log events into a CloudWatch metric, and an alarm on that metric can notify when it crosses 10 in five minutes. S3 event notifications fire on each new log file, not on error counts, threat lists are for GuardDuty IP matching, and insights only group findings.","Domain 1: CloudWatch Logs metric filters",["Threat lists make GuardDuty flag known bad IPs; they do not count unauthorized API calls from any source.","An S3 notification fires whenever a new log file lands, not when error counts cross a threshold.",null,"Insights group findings in Security Hub; CloudTrail errors are not findings and insights do not raise alarms."],2],["scs10",0,1,"An analyst must search 18 months of CloudTrail logs stored in S3 with SQL, without loading them into a database first. Which service fits best?",["Amazon Athena with a table over the log files","CloudTrail Event history in the console","AWS Config advanced queries","Amazon Inspector findings search"],0,"Athena queries data in place in S3 with standard SQL and bills per data scanned; partitioning the table by date keeps queries cheap. Event history only covers 90 days, Config advanced queries search resource configuration, and Inspector deals with vulnerabilities.","Domain 1: analyzing logs",[null,"Event history only covers the last 90 days of management events, not 18 months.","Config advanced queries search the current configuration of resources, not the contents of CloudTrail logs.","Inspector findings are vulnerability results; Inspector cannot search CloudTrail log files."],1],["scs11",0,1,"A company wants logs from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, Route 53 and several third-party tools normalized into one open schema and stored in S3 in its own account for any analytics tool to use. Which service provides this?",["Amazon Detective","AWS Security Hub custom insights","Amazon OpenSearch Service alone","Amazon Security Lake"],3,"Security Lake collects AWS and third-party security data, normalizes it to the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF) in Apache Parquet and stores it in an S3 data lake you own, with subscribers for query or ingestion. Detective builds a behavior graph for investigations, and Security Hub and OpenSearch do not normalize raw logs into OCSF for you.","Domain 1: Amazon Security Lake",["Detective builds a behavior graph for investigations; it does not store normalized logs in your own S3 data lake.","Custom insights group Security Hub findings; they do not collect or normalize raw logs from many sources.","OpenSearch can index logs, but on its own it does not normalize every source to OCSF or give an S3 data lake you own.",null],2],["scs12",0,1,"After someone tightened the bucket policy on the central log bucket in the log archive account, the organization trail stopped delivering logs. What is the most likely fix?",["Turn off Block Public Access on the log bucket","Allow the cloudtrail.amazonaws.com principal s3:PutObject with the trail's aws:SourceArn","Grant every member account's root user write access to the bucket through the bucket policy","Change the trail to deliver to CloudWatch Logs only"],1,"CloudTrail writes as the cloudtrail.amazonaws.com service principal, so the bucket policy must allow it GetBucketAcl and PutObject, ideally scoped with aws:SourceArn. Block Public Access does not affect service principal grants, member root users do not write the logs, and dropping S3 delivery loses the long-term archive.","Domain 1: troubleshooting log delivery",["Block Public Access limits public grants; it does not block a grant to the CloudTrail service principal, so turning it off only adds risk.",null,"CloudTrail writes logs as its service principal, not as member root users, so this grant does not fix delivery and is overly broad.","Dropping S3 delivery loses the long-term, central archive the trail exists to provide."],3],["scs13",0,1,"A trail was changed to encrypt log files with a new customer managed KMS key, and log delivery stopped immediately. What should the engineer check first?",["Whether the S3 bucket has versioning enabled","Whether the key has automatic rotation turned on with a 90-day rotation period","Whether the key policy lets CloudTrail use the key to generate data keys","Whether the trail has Insights events turned on"],2,"CloudTrail needs permission in the KMS key policy (kms:GenerateDataKey* with the trail's context) to encrypt log files; without it, delivery fails. Versioning, rotation and Insights settings do not block log encryption.","Domain 1: KMS permissions for log delivery",["Versioning protects against overwrites but has no effect on whether CloudTrail can encrypt and deliver files.","Rotation settings do not affect permissions; a rotated key keeps working for CloudTrail if the policy allows it.",null,"Insights events are an optional detection feature and do not stop normal log delivery."],3],["scs14",0,1,"During an investigation, analysts need the actual packet contents sent to and from a suspicious EC2 instance, not just connection records. What should they use?",["VPC Traffic Mirroring to an inspection instance","VPC Flow Logs with custom fields","Route 53 Resolver query logs","CloudTrail network activity events"],0,"Traffic Mirroring copies the actual packets from an elastic network interface to a monitoring target for deep inspection. Flow logs, even with custom fields, contain only metadata such as addresses, ports and bytes, and Resolver and CloudTrail logs do not contain packet payloads.","Domain 1: packet-level visibility",[null,"Custom fields add more metadata such as TCP flags, but flow logs never include packet payloads.","Resolver query logs record DNS lookups only, not the content of other network traffic.","CloudTrail records API activity; it does not capture packets flowing to and from an instance."],2],["scs15",0,2,"GuardDuty reports that an IAM user's access key is being used from an unfamiliar IP address to list S3 buckets. What should the responder do first?",["Delete the IAM user and all of its access keys so the credential can never be reused","Deactivate the access key so it stops working but remains for the investigation","Change the user's console password","Remove the user from all IAM groups"],1,"Deactivating the key stops the attacker immediately while keeping the key ID available for CloudTrail searches and possible reactivation. Deleting loses evidence and context, a password change does not affect access keys, and removing groups may leave inline or directly attached policies in place.","Domain 2: compromised access keys",["Deleting the user and keys destroys context and makes it harder to trace and reverse what the attacker did.",null,"The console password is separate from the access key, so the attacker could keep calling the API.","Policies attached directly to the user or inline would still apply, so the key may keep working."],2],["scs16",0,2,"GuardDuty raises an InstanceCredentialExfiltration finding: temporary credentials from an EC2 instance role are being used from outside AWS. Other instances need the role to keep working. What should the responder do?",["Delete the IAM role and create a new one with the same name","Detach the instance profile from every instance that currently uses the role","Rotate the access keys of the IAM user who created the role","Revoke the role's active sessions, which denies tokens issued before now"],3,"Revoke active sessions adds a policy that denies requests from sessions with an aws:TokenIssueTime before the revocation, so stolen tokens stop working while instances fetch new credentials. Deleting the role or detaching profiles breaks healthy instances, and role credentials have no IAM user keys to rotate.","Domain 2: revoking role sessions",["Deleting the role breaks every healthy instance, and a new role with the same name has a new ID that trust relationships may not match.","Detaching profiles stops the healthy instances from working, and the tokens already stolen stay valid until they expire.","Role credentials are temporary and come from STS; there are no IAM user keys involved to rotate.",null],3],["scs17",0,2,"An EC2 instance is believed to be running attacker malware that may exist only in memory. The team wants to keep that evidence. What should it do before stopping the instance?",["Capture a memory image from the running instance","Create an AMI with the default reboot option","Terminate the instance and keep the root volume","Detach and reattach the root EBS volume"],0,"Memory is volatile and lost on stop or reboot, so it must be captured first, followed by EBS snapshots. Creating an AMI with default settings reboots the instance, and terminating or detaching volumes loses the running state.","Domain 2: memory capture",[null,"Creating an AMI with the default option reboots the instance, which wipes memory and any in-memory malware.","Terminating the instance destroys memory and may delete volumes set to delete on termination.","Detaching the root volume requires stopping the instance, which loses memory evidence."],2],["scs18",0,2,"A responder moved a compromised instance to a security group with no inbound or outbound rules, but the attacker's existing SSH session stays open. Why?",["Security groups only apply to new instances","Network ACLs override security groups with an allow","Tracked connections already in progress are not interrupted by a security group change","The default security group of the VPC is always evaluated as well and allows the session"],2,"Security groups are stateful, and existing tracked connections can continue after rules are removed. To cut them, AWS guidance is to deny the traffic with a network ACL or first make the flows untracked before applying the isolation group. Security groups apply to running instances, and there is no automatic default-group evaluation.","Domain 2: isolating EC2 instances",["Security groups apply to running instances as soon as they are attached, not only to new instances.","Network ACLs and security groups are both evaluated; an ACL allow does not override a security group.",null,"Only the security groups attached to the network interface are evaluated; the default group is not added automatically."],3],["scs19",0,2,"Investigators want a visual timeline of how a suspicious role, the IP addresses it used and the instances it touched behaved over the past weeks, built from existing logs. Which service provides this?",["AWS Config timeline","Amazon Detective","AWS Trusted Advisor","Amazon Macie"],1,"Detective automatically builds a behavior graph from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, GuardDuty findings and other sources, and shows entity activity over time for scoping. Config shows configuration changes, not behavior, while Trusted Advisor and Macie do different jobs.","Domain 2: Amazon Detective",["Config shows how a resource's configuration changed, not which IPs and roles interacted over time.",null,"Trusted Advisor gives best-practice checks and does not analyze activity for investigations.","Macie finds sensitive data in S3 and does not build activity timelines for roles and IPs."],2],["scs20",0,2,"When GuardDuty reports a high-severity finding on an EC2 instance, the company wants the instance isolated, snapshotted and tagged automatically, with each step retried if it fails. What design fits?",["A CloudWatch dashboard reviewed hourly by the SOC","A Security Hub insight filtered by severity","An AWS Config rule that marks the instance noncompliant","An EventBridge rule that starts a Step Functions workflow"],3,"EventBridge receives GuardDuty findings and can start a Step Functions state machine that runs each step with retries and error handling. Dashboards and insights need people to act, and a Config rule reports compliance but does not respond to findings.","Domain 2: automated response",["A dashboard needs a person to notice and act, so isolation could be delayed by an hour or more.","Insights group findings for review; they do not take any automated action.","A Config rule evaluates configuration compliance; it does not react to GuardDuty findings or run response steps.",null],2],["scs21",0,2,"Security Hub produces many low-value findings from a sandbox account with a known exception. The team wants them marked suppressed automatically as they arrive, without code. What should it use?",["Security Hub automation rules","A GuardDuty trusted IP list","An SCP that denies securityhub:BatchImportFindings","A Lambda function triggered every hour"],0,"Automation rules update or suppress findings that match criteria such as account ID and control as soon as Security Hub receives them. A trusted IP list is a GuardDuty feature, blocking imports would lose all findings, and a scheduled Lambda is custom code with a delay.","Domain 2: Security Hub automation rules",[null,"Trusted IP lists stop GuardDuty from generating findings for listed IPs; they do not manage Security Hub findings.","Blocking imports would stop all findings arriving, including the ones that matter.","A scheduled function needs custom code and leaves findings unsuppressed until it runs."],2],["scs22",0,2,"The company wants any S3 bucket that becomes publicly readable to be fixed automatically, with a record of each fix. What is the most suitable approach?",["A daily manual review of the S3 console","An IAM permissions boundary for all developers","An AWS Config rule with automatic remediation using a Systems Manager Automation runbook","A CloudFront distribution in front of every bucket with origin access control turned on for all objects"],2,"Config detects the noncompliant bucket and automatic remediation runs a Systems Manager Automation document to block public access, with history in Config. Manual reviews are slow, a boundary does not fix existing buckets, and CloudFront does not remove a public bucket policy.","Domain 2: Config remediation",["A daily manual review is slow and leaves the bucket exposed for up to a day.","A permissions boundary limits future actions by developers but does not fix buckets that are already public.",null,"Putting CloudFront in front does not remove the public bucket policy or ACL; the bucket stays public directly."],1],["scs23",0,2,"Disk images and logs collected during an incident may be used in court. They must be stored so they cannot be changed or deleted for seven years. What should the team use?",["An S3 bucket with lifecycle rules that move the evidence to Glacier Deep Archive","An S3 bucket in a forensics account with Object Lock in compliance mode","An EBS volume with snapshots copied daily","An S3 bucket with SSE-KMS and a strict key policy"],1,"Object Lock in compliance mode makes each object version write-once-read-many until retention ends, and keeping it in an isolated forensics account limits who can reach it. Lifecycle rules and encryption do not stop deletion, and EBS snapshots can be deleted by administrators.","Domain 2: evidence handling",["Moving evidence to Deep Archive lowers cost but does not stop anyone with permission from deleting it.",null,"EBS snapshots can be deleted by administrators and are not write-once storage.","Encryption protects confidentiality; a principal with delete permission can still remove the objects."],2],["scs24",0,2,"Engineers sign in only through IAM Identity Center with an external identity provider. The team worries about losing all access if that provider fails during an incident. What should it prepare?",["An SCP that allows all actions for the security team","A shared IAM user for the whole team whose password is stored in a team chat channel","Access keys for every engineer stored on their laptops","Break-glass IAM users with hardware MFA, sealed credentials and alarms on every use"],3,"Break-glass accounts are a small number of tightly protected IAM users with strong MFA whose credentials are stored securely and whose use triggers alerts and review. SCPs never grant permissions, shared credentials in chat are insecure, and long-term keys on laptops create new risk.","Domain 2: break-glass access",["SCPs never grant permissions, and they would not help if nobody can sign in at all.","A shared password in chat has no accountability and is easy to leak.","Long-term keys on laptops add a large new attack surface and are hard to track.",null],3],["scs25",0,2,"The team has written runbooks for GuardDuty findings but has never tested them. What is the best way to check they work before a real incident?",["Run a game day in a non-production account using GuardDuty sample findings","Ask an external auditor to read the runbooks","Wait for the next real finding in production and measure how long the response takes","Convert the runbooks into a PDF and store them in S3"],0,"Game days and simulations exercise people, permissions and automation under realistic conditions, and GuardDuty sample findings can trigger the same event paths safely. Reading or storing documents does not test them, and learning during a real incident is too late.","Domain 2: testing the plan",[null,"Reading documents does not show whether permissions, tools and people actually work under pressure.","Discovering broken steps during a real incident costs time and increases damage.","Storing the runbooks as a PDF preserves them but does not test them."],1],["scs26",0,2,"An attacker had shell access to an application server whose role could read a database password from Secrets Manager. The server has been contained. What should the team do next to recover safely?",["Increase the database instance size","Delete the Secrets Manager secret and hard-code a new password","Rotate the database secret immediately and review database logins","Enable Multi-AZ on the database"],2,"Anything the attacker could read must be treated as compromised, so the secret should be rotated, and database access logs reviewed for misuse. Hard-coding a password creates a new weakness, and resizing or Multi-AZ do not address the stolen credential.","Domain 2: recovery",["A larger instance does nothing about the password the attacker may have read.","Deleting the secret and hard-coding a password spreads the credential into code and removes rotation.",null,"Multi-AZ improves availability but does not address a stolen database credential."],2],["scs27",0,2,"Responders must find every API call made across 40 accounts in the last six months by one access key ID, using SQL. The organization already sends events to a CloudTrail Lake event data store. What should they do?",["Search Event history in each account one by one","Run a SQL query in CloudTrail Lake filtered on the access key ID","Open Amazon Inspector and filter findings","Export AWS Config snapshots from every account and search them for the access key"],1,"CloudTrail Lake stores organization events in an event data store that can be queried with SQL, so one query can filter on the access key across accounts. Event history is per account and per Region and limited to 90 days, and Inspector and Config do not hold API activity.","Domain 2: CloudTrail Lake",["Event history is per account and per Region and only covers 90 days, so it cannot reach six months back.",null,"Inspector holds vulnerability findings, not API activity by access key.","Config snapshots describe resource configuration and do not contain API calls or access key IDs."],2],["scs28",0,3,"A login page behind an Application Load Balancer is being hit by thousands of password guesses per minute from a few IP addresses. What AWS WAF feature best limits this?",["A rate-based rule that blocks IPs over a request threshold","A geo match rule that blocks all other countries","A size constraint rule on the request body","An IP set that allows only the office network"],0,"Rate-based rules count requests per source (or other key) over a time window and block sources over the limit, which directly slows credential stuffing. Geo blocking and size limits are not aimed at request floods, and allowing only the office would block real customers.","Domain 3: AWS WAF rate-based rules",[null,"Blocking other countries also blocks real customers and does not stop attackers in allowed countries.","Password guesses are small requests, so a size constraint would not catch them.","Allowing only the office network would lock out every real customer of a public login page."],1],["scs29",0,3,"An API behind API Gateway must be protected against SQL injection with minimal rule writing. What should the engineer add?",["A security group rule on the API Gateway endpoint","A Network Firewall stateless rule that allows only port 443 to the API","An AWS WAF web ACL with the AWS managed SQL database rule group","A Shield Advanced protection for the API"],2,"AWS managed rule groups for WAF include rules that detect SQL injection patterns, and web ACLs can attach to API Gateway stages. API Gateway does not use security groups, a stateless port rule cannot see SQL in requests, and Shield focuses on DDoS.","Domain 3: WAF managed rule groups",["API Gateway endpoints do not use security groups, and ports cannot detect SQL in a request.","A stateless port rule cannot see SQL injection inside an HTTPS request, and API Gateway is not placed behind Network Firewall this way.",null,"Shield Advanced focuses on DDoS; it does not inspect requests for SQL injection by itself."],1],["scs30",0,3,"A company running a large public site wants access to the AWS Shield Response Team during attacks and credit for scaling charges caused by DDoS. What should it subscribe to?",["AWS Shield Standard","AWS WAF Bot Control","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Shield Advanced"],3,"Shield Advanced adds 24/7 access to the Shield Response Team, DDoS cost protection, enhanced detection and more. Shield Standard is automatic and free but has no response team or cost protection, and Bot Control and GuardDuty are not DDoS services.","Domain 3: Shield Advanced",["Shield Standard is automatic and free but has no response team access or DDoS cost protection.","Bot Control manages automated bot traffic; it does not give access to the Shield Response Team.","GuardDuty detects threats in accounts; it does not protect a site against DDoS or cover scaling charges.",null],1],["scs31",0,3,"Static content in an S3 bucket encrypted with SSE-KMS must be served only through CloudFront, never directly from S3. What should the engineer use?",["A public-read bucket policy with a condition that checks the Referer header value","Origin access control with a bucket policy for the CloudFront service principal","An S3 website endpoint with a custom domain","Signed URLs issued by the S3 bucket"],1,"Origin access control signs CloudFront's requests to S3, supports SSE-KMS, and the bucket policy allows only cloudfront.amazonaws.com for that distribution. Referer headers are easy to fake, website endpoints must be public, and S3 presigned URLs would bypass CloudFront.","Domain 3: CloudFront origin access control",["The bucket would still be public, and Referer headers are easy to fake.",null,"Website endpoints require public access and do not support SSE-KMS objects through CloudFront this way.","S3 presigned URLs send users directly to S3, bypassing CloudFront completely."],2],["scs32",0,3,"Paying subscribers must be able to download hundreds of video files from a CloudFront distribution without the site generating a new link for each file. What should be used?",["CloudFront signed cookies","CloudFront signed URLs","A geo restriction on the distribution","An AWS WAF IP set rule"],0,"Signed cookies grant access to many files, such as everything under a path, with one credential. Signed URLs are per file, while geo restriction and IP sets cannot identify paying users.","Domain 3: signed URLs vs signed cookies",[null,"Signed URLs work per file, so the site would have to generate a new URL for each of hundreds of files.","Geo restriction limits countries; it cannot tell paying subscribers from anyone else.","IP set rules cannot identify subscribers, whose IP addresses change constantly."],2],["scs33",0,3,"A single IP address range is attacking several instances in one subnet. The team wants to block that range for the whole subnet while leaving all other traffic unchanged. What should it change?",["Add a deny rule to each instance's security group","Remove the internet gateway from the VPC","Create a new route table without a default route","Add a deny rule for the range to the subnet's network ACL"],3,"Network ACLs apply to the whole subnet and support explicit deny rules evaluated in number order. Security groups have allow rules only, and removing the internet gateway or default route would cut off all traffic, not just the attackers.","Domain 3: security groups vs network ACLs",["Security groups only support allow rules, so a deny rule cannot be added to them.","Removing the internet gateway cuts off all internet traffic, not just the attacking range.","A route table without a default route breaks all internet traffic for the subnet.",null],1],["scs34",0,3,"Workloads in several VPCs may only reach an approved list of internet domains, and all egress traffic must pass through stateful intrusion prevention rules. Which service meets this?",["Security groups with outbound rules","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Network Firewall in a central inspection VPC","Network ACLs on every subnet"],2,"Network Firewall provides stateful inspection, domain allow lists and Suricata-compatible IPS rules, and can be shared through a central inspection VPC with Transit Gateway. Security groups and network ACLs work on IP addresses and ports, not domains, and GuardDuty detects but does not block.","Domain 3: AWS Network Firewall",["Security groups filter by IP address and port; they cannot allow traffic by domain name or run IPS rules.","GuardDuty detects threats but does not block traffic or enforce an allow list.",null,"Network ACLs are stateless IP and port filters; they cannot filter by domain or inspect traffic."],2],["scs35",0,3,"The team wants to stop instances in a VPC from resolving known malware and DNS tunneling domains, whatever IP addresses those domains use. What should it deploy?",["Network ACL deny rules for the domains' current IP addresses","Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall with a blocking rule group","A Route 53 private hosted zone for each domain","A GuardDuty threat list with the domain names"],1,"DNS Firewall filters queries sent to the Route 53 Resolver, blocking or alerting on listed domains, including AWS managed domain lists. IP-based rules break when addresses change, private hosted zones are a workaround without logging or managed lists, and threat lists only affect GuardDuty findings.","Domain 3: DNS Firewall",["Malicious domains change IP addresses often, so IP deny rules quickly go stale and miss tunneling.",null,"Private hosted zones can override names but give no managed threat lists and are a fragile workaround.","GuardDuty threat lists affect findings only; they do not block DNS resolution."],2],["scs36",0,3,"Instances in a private subnet must reach S3 without internet access, and only the company's own buckets should be reachable through that path. What should the engineer configure?",["A gateway VPC endpoint for S3 with an endpoint policy listing the company's buckets","A NAT gateway in a public subnet with a security group that allows outbound port 443","An internet gateway with a network ACL for S3 prefixes","A VPC peering connection to the S3 service"],0,"A gateway endpoint gives private access to S3 through the route table, and its endpoint policy can restrict which buckets can be reached, which helps prevent data exfiltration to other buckets. NAT and internet gateways send traffic over the internet path, and you cannot peer with S3.","Domain 3: VPC endpoints and endpoint policies",[null,"A NAT gateway sends traffic toward S3 over the public path and cannot restrict which buckets are reached.","An internet gateway gives the subnet internet access, which the requirement rules out.","VPC peering connects two VPCs; you cannot peer with the S3 service."],2],["scs37",0,3,"A bucket holding confidential data must accept requests only when they arrive through one specific VPC endpoint. Which bucket policy condition supports this?",["aws:SourceIp with the VPC's private CIDR range in an Allow for the company's roles","aws:PrincipalOrgID with the organization ID","aws:SecureTransport set to true","aws:SourceVpce with the endpoint ID in a Deny for all other requests"],3,"aws:SourceVpce holds the ID of the VPC endpoint a request came through, so a Deny where it does not match blocks every other path. aws:SourceIp does not contain private addresses for endpoint traffic, while the org ID and TLS conditions do not limit the network path.","Domain 3: endpoint conditions",["Requests through a VPC endpoint do not carry a public source IP, and aws:SourceIp does not hold private VPC addresses, so this check fails.","aws:PrincipalOrgID checks who is calling, not which network path the request used.","aws:SecureTransport only checks for TLS; it does not tie requests to one endpoint.",null],3],["scs38",0,3,"A bank uses a 10 Gbps dedicated Direct Connect connection and must encrypt traffic at layer 2 between its router and the AWS device. What should it use?",["A Site-to-Site VPN over the public internet","AWS Client VPN","MACsec on the Direct Connect connection","TLS termination on a Network Load Balancer"],2,"MACsec provides layer-2 encryption on supported dedicated Direct Connect connections. A Site-to-Site VPN encrypts at layer 3 with IPsec, Client VPN is for individual users, and NLB TLS only protects specific application flows.","Domain 3: Direct Connect encryption",["A VPN over the internet does not use the Direct Connect link and encrypts at layer 3, not layer 2.","Client VPN connects individual user devices, not a data center router over Direct Connect.",null,"NLB TLS protects individual application connections, not all traffic on the Direct Connect link."],2],["scs39",0,3,"Employees need access to internal web applications from anywhere, with each request checked against their identity and device security posture, and without a VPN client. Which service fits?",["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS Verified Access","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon Cognito user pools"],1,"Verified Access provides zero trust access to applications, evaluating each request against identity and device trust providers without a VPN. Site-to-Site VPN connects networks, Transit Gateway routes between networks, and Cognito handles customer sign-in, not device posture.","Domain 3: Verified Access",["Site-to-Site VPN connects networks and does not check each request against user identity and device posture.",null,"Transit Gateway routes traffic between VPCs and networks; it does not authorize individual users.","Cognito user pools sign in application customers and do not evaluate device security posture."],2],["scs40",0,3,"The security team wants administrators to get shell access to EC2 instances with no inbound ports open, no SSH keys, IAM-based authorization and a log of every session. What should be used?",["AWS Systems Manager Session Manager","A bastion host in a public subnet","EC2 serial console for all users","SSH with keys stored in Secrets Manager"],0,"Session Manager uses the SSM Agent's outbound connection, is controlled by IAM, needs no open inbound ports or keys, and can log sessions to S3 or CloudWatch Logs. Bastions and SSH need open ports and keys, and the serial console is meant for troubleshooting.","Domain 3: Session Manager",[null,"A bastion needs an open SSH port and keys, which the requirements rule out.","The serial console is for troubleshooting boot and network problems, not everyday administration for all users.","SSH still needs an open inbound port, and keys in Secrets Manager are still keys to manage."],1],["scs41",0,3,"A web application on EC2 had a server-side request forgery (SSRF) flaw that an attacker could use to fetch the instance role's credentials from the metadata service. What instance setting reduces this risk?",["Assign a larger instance type","Remove the instance's public IP address","Turn on detailed CloudWatch monitoring","Require IMDSv2 session tokens on the instance"],3,"IMDSv2 requires a token obtained with a PUT request and custom header, which typical SSRF flaws and open proxies cannot send. Instance size, public IP and monitoring do not change how the metadata service answers requests from the instance itself.","Domain 3: IMDSv2",["Instance size does not change how the metadata service responds to requests.","The SSRF request comes from the instance itself, so removing the public IP does not protect the metadata service.","Detailed monitoring only adds metrics; it does not block credential theft.",null],2],["scs42",0,3,"The company wants continuous vulnerability scanning of its EC2 instances, container images pushed to Amazon ECR and Lambda functions, with findings sent to Security Hub. Which service should it enable?",["Amazon Macie","AWS Config","Amazon Inspector","Amazon Detective"],2,"Inspector scans EC2, ECR images and Lambda functions for software vulnerabilities and network exposure and sends findings to Security Hub and EventBridge. Macie finds sensitive data, Config tracks configuration, and Detective supports investigations.","Domain 3: Amazon Inspector",["Macie finds sensitive data in S3; it does not scan workloads for vulnerabilities.","Config tracks resource configuration and compliance, not software vulnerabilities in images or functions.",null,"Detective helps investigate security findings; it does not scan for vulnerabilities."],1],["scs43",0,3,"An application instance cannot reach a database instance in another subnet, and the team does not know which security group, network ACL or route table is blocking it. What tool helps most?",["AWS Trusted Advisor","VPC Reachability Analyzer","CloudTrail Event history","AWS Config conformance packs"],1,"Reachability Analyzer performs static analysis of the path between a source and destination and names the component that blocks it. Trusted Advisor gives general checks, CloudTrail shows API calls, and conformance packs check compliance rules, not paths.","Domain 3: network troubleshooting",["Trusted Advisor runs general best-practice checks and does not trace a specific network path.",null,"Event history shows API calls, which will not reveal which rule blocks traffic.","Conformance packs evaluate compliance rules; they do not analyze reachability between two resources."],1],["scs44",0,4,"In one account, a role's identity policy allows s3:GetObject on a bucket. The bucket policy does not mention the role, and no other policies apply. What happens when the role reads an object?",["The request is allowed because an allow in one policy is enough within the same account","The request is denied because the bucket policy must also explicitly name the role as a principal","The request is denied unless an SCP allows it explicitly","The request is allowed only if the object is public"],0,"Within the same account, an allow in either the identity-based policy or the resource-based policy is enough when nothing denies it. The bucket policy does not have to name the role, and the object does not need to be public.","Domain 4: policy evaluation",[null,"Within one account, the bucket policy does not need to name the role if the identity policy allows the action.","SCPs never grant access; with the default FullAWSAccess SCP in place, no extra SCP allow is needed.","Objects do not need to be public; an IAM allow is enough for principals in the same account."],2],["scs45",0,4,"A role in account A has an identity policy allowing s3:GetObject on a bucket in account B. The bucket policy in account B says nothing about account A. What happens?",["The request is allowed because the identity policy grants it","The request is allowed if both accounts are in the same Region","The request is denied because account B's bucket policy must also allow it","The request is denied because roles cannot access resources that belong to other accounts"],2,"For cross-account access both sides must agree: the caller's identity policy and the resource policy in the owning account. Regions do not matter, and roles can reach other accounts when the resource policy allows it or through role assumption.","Domain 4: cross-account access",["Across accounts, the identity policy alone is not enough; the bucket owner must also allow access.","Regions have nothing to do with it; cross-account access is decided by policies on both sides.",null,"Roles can access resources in other accounts when the resource policy allows them."],3],["scs46",0,4,"Developers must be able to create IAM roles for their Lambda functions, but must never create a role with more permissions than a set limit. What should the security team use?",["A service control policy that denies iam:CreateRole and iam:PutRolePolicy in the account","A required permissions boundary, enforced with an iam:PermissionsBoundary condition","An IAM group with the AdministratorAccess policy","A Cognito identity pool for the developers"],1,"Requiring developers to attach a specific permissions boundary to any role they create caps that role's effective permissions. Denying CreateRole blocks the work entirely, AdministratorAccess removes the limit, and Cognito is for application users.","Domain 4: permissions boundaries",["Denying role creation stops developers from doing the work they need, rather than limiting how powerful roles can be.",null,"AdministratorAccess removes any limit, which is the opposite of the requirement.","Cognito identity pools give application users credentials; they do not limit roles developers create."],3],["scs47",0,4,"Hundreds of project teams share one account. Engineers should only manage EC2 instances tagged with their own project, and new projects should not need policy changes. What approach fits?",["A separate IAM policy for each project that lists the instance IDs the team may manage","A resource-based policy on every EC2 instance","A session policy passed by each engineer","An ABAC policy comparing aws:ResourceTag/project with aws:PrincipalTag/project"],3,"Attribute-based access control compares a principal's tag with the resource's tag, so one policy scales to any number of projects. Per-project policies need constant updates, EC2 instances do not support resource-based policies, and session policies are set by the caller.","Domain 4: ABAC",["Per-project policies with instance IDs need constant editing as projects and instances change.","EC2 instances do not support resource-based policies.","Session policies are set by the caller and can only restrict, so they cannot enforce project boundaries across all engineers.",null],3],["scs48",0,4,"A policy must allow deleting production snapshots only when the caller authenticated with MFA. Which condition key supports this?",["aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent","aws:SecureTransport","aws:PrincipalOrgID","aws:RequestedRegion"],0,"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent is true when the temporary credentials were obtained with MFA. The other keys check TLS, organization membership and Region, none of which prove MFA.","Domain 4: condition keys",[null,"aws:SecureTransport checks for TLS on the request, not whether the user signed in with MFA.","aws:PrincipalOrgID checks organization membership, not MFA use.","aws:RequestedRegion checks which Region the call targets, not how the user authenticated."],1],["scs49",0,4,"A SaaS monitoring vendor assumes a role in many customer accounts. The customer wants to stop another vendor customer from tricking the vendor into accessing this account. What should the trust policy include?",["A condition on aws:SourceIp for the vendor's office","A permissions boundary on the role","A condition requiring a unique sts:ExternalId","An MFA condition for the vendor's service"],2,"A unique external ID, agreed with the vendor and required in the trust policy, prevents the confused deputy problem. Source IP checks can break with vendor infrastructure, a boundary limits permissions but not who assumes the role, and automated services cannot use MFA.","Domain 4: external IDs",["Vendor IP addresses can change, and a source IP check does not stop the vendor being tricked into acting for another customer.","A boundary limits the role's permissions but not who can assume the role or on whose behalf.",null,"Automated vendor services assume roles programmatically and cannot present MFA."],3],["scs50",0,4,"An SNS topic policy allows the s3.amazonaws.com service to publish so that bucket events can be delivered. What should be added so that buckets in other accounts cannot use this permission?",["A Deny statement for all principals except the account root user and the S3 service","Conditions on aws:SourceArn and aws:SourceAccount for the company's bucket","A permissions boundary on the SNS topic","Server-side encryption on the topic"],1,"When a service principal acts for a resource, aws:SourceArn and aws:SourceAccount make sure only your resource can trigger the action, which prevents cross-service confused deputy attacks. Boundaries do not attach to topics, and encryption does not control who can publish.","Domain 4: confused deputy with service principals",["This statement would block S3 itself from publishing and still not tie the permission to the company's own bucket.",null,"Permissions boundaries attach to IAM users and roles, not to SNS topics.","Encryption protects message contents but does not control who is allowed to publish."],3],["scs51",0,4,"A company wants employees to sign in to 30 AWS accounts with its existing external identity provider, and wants users and groups created in AWS automatically when HR adds them to that provider. What should it configure?",["An IAM SAML identity provider and matching roles created in each of the 30 accounts","Amazon Cognito user pools with a hosted UI","AWS Directory Service Simple AD","IAM Identity Center with SAML 2.0 federation and SCIM provisioning"],3,"Identity Center federates with an external IdP over SAML 2.0 and uses SCIM to sync users and groups automatically, then maps groups to permission sets in each account. Per-account SAML providers do not scale, Cognito is for application users, and Simple AD is a separate directory.","Domain 4: IAM Identity Center",["Per-account SAML providers and roles do not scale to 30 accounts and give no automatic user and group provisioning.","Cognito user pools are for customer-facing apps, not workforce access to AWS accounts.","Simple AD is a separate, basic directory that would not sync from the existing IdP.",null],2],["scs52",0,4,"In IAM Identity Center, an auditors group needs read-only access to 12 accounts. What is the correct way to grant it?",["Create a read-only permission set and assign the group to the 12 accounts","Create 12 IAM users for each auditor","Attach the ReadOnlyAccess managed policy directly to the Identity Center instance","Share the management account's credentials with the auditors"],0,"A permission set defines the permissions, and assigning it to a group for specific accounts creates a matching IAM role in each account. IAM users defeat central access, policies are not attached to the Identity Center instance, and sharing management credentials is dangerous.","Domain 4: permission sets",[null,"Creating IAM users per auditor per account multiplies long-term credentials and bypasses central access.","Policies are not attached to the Identity Center instance; permission sets carry them into accounts.","Sharing management account credentials gives auditors far too much power and no accountability."],1],["scs53",0,4,"A mobile app's users sign in through a Cognito user pool and then need temporary AWS credentials to upload files to their own S3 prefix. What provides the credentials?",["The user pool's app client secret","IAM access keys created for each user and embedded in the mobile app at sign-in","A Cognito identity pool mapped to an IAM role with a policy variable","A Lambda authorizer on API Gateway"],2,"Identity pools exchange the user pool token for temporary AWS credentials, and a policy variable such as the identity ID can limit each user to their own prefix. App client secrets and Lambda authorizers do not issue AWS credentials, and per-user IAM keys are unsafe and do not scale.","Domain 4: Cognito user pools vs identity pools",["An app client secret identifies the app to the user pool; it does not give AWS credentials, and mobile apps should not hold secrets.","Access keys embedded in an app can be extracted by anyone and are long-term credentials.",null,"A Lambda authorizer decides API Gateway access; it does not issue AWS credentials for direct S3 uploads."],2],["scs54",0,4,"A document-sharing application needs fine-grained rules such as 'editors can change documents in their own folder', managed outside the code in a policy language. Which AWS service fits?",["IAM Access Analyzer","Amazon Verified Permissions","AWS Resource Access Manager","IAM Identity Center"],1,"Verified Permissions stores and evaluates application authorization policies written in the Cedar language. Access Analyzer reviews IAM access, RAM shares AWS resources between accounts, and Identity Center manages workforce access to AWS.","Domain 4: Verified Permissions",["Access Analyzer reviews IAM and resource policies for AWS access, not in-application permissions.",null,"Resource Access Manager shares AWS resources between accounts; it does not evaluate app rules.","Identity Center manages workforce access to AWS accounts and apps, not fine-grained rules inside an app."],2],["scs55",0,4,"On-premises servers run scripts that call AWS APIs. They have X.509 certificates from the company's certificate authority, and the security team bans long-term access keys. What should be used?",["An IAM user per server with keys rotated monthly","Amazon Cognito identity pools","AWS Directory Service AD Connector","IAM Roles Anywhere with a trust anchor for the company CA"],3,"Roles Anywhere lets workloads outside AWS exchange a certificate from a trusted CA for temporary role credentials. IAM user keys are long-term, Cognito serves app users, and AD Connector connects directories, not servers to AWS APIs.","Domain 4: IAM Roles Anywhere",["Rotated keys are still long-term access keys, which the team has banned.","Cognito identity pools serve application end users, not servers calling AWS APIs.","AD Connector links an existing directory to AWS; it does not give servers AWS credentials.",null],2],["scs56",0,4,"A GitHub Actions pipeline deploys to AWS using stored access keys. How should the team remove those keys?",["An OIDC identity provider and a role whose trust policy checks the token's sub claim","Move the access keys into encrypted repository secrets and rotate them every 90 days","Use the root user's keys only for the deployment job","Create a Cognito user pool for the pipeline"],0,"With OIDC federation the pipeline calls AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity and gets short-lived credentials, and conditions on the sub claim limit which repositories and branches can assume the role. Encrypted secrets are still long-term keys, root keys are never appropriate, and Cognito is not for CI/CD.","Domain 4: OIDC federation for pipelines",[null,"Encrypted secrets are still long-term access keys, just stored differently.","Root user keys give unlimited access and should never be used for automation.","Cognito user pools sign in people to applications; they are not used for pipeline federation."],3],["scs57",0,4,"A company with 200 member accounts wants no member account to have a usable root password or root access keys, while still letting the security team perform the few tasks that need root. What should it use?",["An SCP that denies all actions by root users","A password policy that expires root passwords after 30 days","Centralized root access management in IAM","Hardware MFA devices for each member account root user"],2,"Centralized root access lets the management or delegated account remove member root credentials and run privileged root tasks with short-term sessions when needed. An SCP limits root but leaves credentials in place, password policies do not apply to root, and 200 MFA devices are hard to manage.","Domain 4: root user protection",["An SCP limits what root can do but leaves the root password and keys in place.","Password policies do not apply to the root user, and rotation does not remove the credential.",null,"Two hundred MFA devices are hard to manage, and root passwords still exist in every account."],3],["scs58",0,4,"The security team wants a list of IAM roles that have not been used in 90 days and permissions that roles never use, across the organization. What should it enable?",["The IAM credential report","AWS Trusted Advisor cost checks","CloudTrail Insights events","An IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer"],3,"Unused access analyzers report unused roles, access keys, passwords and unused permissions across accounts. Credential reports cover IAM users only, Trusted Advisor cost checks are unrelated, and Insights detects unusual API volumes.","Domain 4: unused access findings",["The credential report covers IAM users and their credentials, not roles or unused permissions.","Trusted Advisor cost checks look for idle resources, not unused IAM roles and permissions.","Insights detects unusual API call volume, not unused access.",null],2],["scs59",0,4,"The company must find any S3 bucket, KMS key or IAM role trust policy that grants access to principals outside its AWS organization. What should it use?",["IAM last-accessed information","An Access Analyzer external access analyzer with the organization as the zone of trust","Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery","AWS Config advanced queries run across all accounts to list every resource-based policy"],1,"External access analyzers read resource-based policies and report any that allow access from outside the zone of trust. Last-accessed data shows usage, Macie looks for sensitive data, and Config queries do not reason about policy logic.","Domain 4: external access findings",["Last-accessed data shows when services were used, not which resource policies grant outside access.",null,"Macie looks for sensitive data content, not for policies that share resources externally.","Config queries list configurations but do not reason about whether policy logic grants access to outsiders."],2],["scs60",0,4,"A developer receives: \"not authorized to perform: ec2:RunInstances ... with an explicit deny in a service control policy\". The developer's IAM policy allows RunInstances. Where is the fix?",["In the SCP attached to the account or its OU in AWS Organizations","In the developer's identity policy, by adding an explicit allow for ec2:RunInstances","In the EC2 instance's security group","In the KMS key policy for EBS encryption"],0,"The error names the policy type that denied the request. An explicit deny in an SCP overrides any allow in the account, so it must be changed in Organizations. Editing the identity policy cannot override it, and security groups or key policies are not mentioned.","Domain 4: troubleshooting access denied",[null,"An explicit deny in an SCP overrides any allow, so adding another allow in the identity policy cannot fix it.","Security groups filter network traffic; they have no effect on API authorization.","The error names a service control policy, not a KMS key policy."],2],["scs61",0,4,"An application role has broad permissions from its first release. The team wants a least-privilege policy based on what the application actually did over the last 30 days. What should it use?",["The IAM policy simulator","AWS Config rule history","IAM Access Analyzer policy generation from CloudTrail activity","A service control policy generated by AWS Control Tower for the application account"],2,"Access Analyzer can generate a policy from the role's CloudTrail history, listing the services and actions it used, which you then review and tighten. The simulator tests existing policies, Config tracks configuration, and SCPs are guardrails, not role policies.","Domain 4: policy generation",["The simulator tests how existing policies behave; it does not write a new policy from past activity.","Config history tracks resource configuration, not which API actions a role called.",null,"SCPs are account guardrails; they do not create least-privilege policies for a role."],1],["scs62",0,5,"An Application Load Balancer must accept only TLS 1.2 or later with strong ciphers. What should the engineer change?",["The target group's health check protocol","The HTTPS listener's security policy","The security group of the load balancer","The ACM certificate's key algorithm"],1,"ELB security policies define the TLS protocol versions and ciphers the listener negotiates. Health checks, security groups and certificate key type do not control which TLS versions are accepted.","Domain 5: ELB security policies",["The health check protocol affects how targets are tested, not what TLS versions clients may use.",null,"Security groups filter ports and IP addresses; they cannot choose TLS versions or ciphers.","The certificate's key type does not decide which TLS protocol versions the listener accepts."],1],["scs63",0,5,"Every request to a bucket that does not use TLS must be rejected, whatever other policies say. Which statement belongs in the bucket policy?",["Allow s3:* for all principals with a condition that aws:SecureTransport is true","Deny s3:* when aws:SourceVpce is present","Allow s3:GetObject for the aws:PrincipalOrgID","Deny s3:* for all principals when aws:SecureTransport is false"],3,"A Deny on aws:SecureTransport false overrides any allow, so plain HTTP requests fail everywhere. An Allow with the condition does not stop other allows from permitting HTTP, and the endpoint and org conditions have nothing to do with TLS.","Domain 5: enforcing encryption in transit",["An Allow with a condition does not stop other statements from allowing plain HTTP requests.","Denying requests through a VPC endpoint has nothing to do with TLS and could block legitimate traffic.","An organization-based allow says nothing about encryption in transit.",null],1],["scs64",0,5,"An application must encrypt 500 MB files with a KMS key. Why does it call GenerateDataKey instead of sending the files to KMS Encrypt?",["KMS Encrypt handles at most 4 KB, so large data needs envelope encryption","GenerateDataKey is the only KMS call that is recorded in CloudTrail for auditing","KMS Encrypt works only with asymmetric keys","Data keys never leave KMS, so they are safer"],0,"KMS encrypts at most 4 KB directly, so large data is encrypted locally with a data key that KMS returns both in plaintext and encrypted. All KMS calls are logged, Encrypt works with symmetric keys too, and data keys do leave KMS; that is the point.","Domain 5: envelope encryption",[null,"Every KMS API call, including Encrypt, is recorded in CloudTrail, so logging is not the reason.","KMS Encrypt works with symmetric keys, which are the default key type.","GenerateDataKey returns the data key to the caller, so data keys do leave KMS; that is how envelope encryption works."],2],["scs65",0,5,"A service needs to use a KMS key temporarily for a single job and then lose access, without anyone editing the key policy each time. What KMS feature fits?",["A multi-Region replica key","An alias pointing to the key","A grant that can be retired when the job completes","Imported key material with an expiration date"],2,"Grants delegate specific key operations to a principal programmatically and can be retired or revoked afterwards, which is how services such as EBS use keys. Aliases are names, replica keys are for other Regions, and expiring key material affects everyone using the key.","Domain 5: KMS grants",["Replica keys are for using the same key in other Regions, not for temporary delegation.","An alias is only a friendly name for a key and grants no access.",null,"Expiring imported key material makes the key unusable for everyone, not just the job."],2],["scs66",0,5,"A role in account B must decrypt data with a customer managed KMS key in account A. What configuration is required?",["Only an IAM policy in account B that allows kms:Decrypt on the key's full ARN in account A","Account A's key policy allows account B, and an IAM policy in B allows the role","A resource share of the key through AWS RAM","Copying the key to account B with the CreateKey API"],1,"Cross-account KMS use needs both the key policy in the owning account and an IAM policy in the caller's account. An IAM policy alone is not enough, KMS keys are not shared through RAM, and key material cannot be copied by creating a key.","Domain 5: cross-account KMS",["The key policy in account A must also allow account B; an IAM policy in B alone is not enough.",null,"KMS keys are shared through key policies and grants, not through AWS RAM resource shares.","Key material cannot be copied by creating a key; CreateKey makes a new, unrelated key."],3],["scs67",0,5,"An administrator with kms:* in his IAM policy is denied when he tries to use a new customer managed key. The key policy lists only one key administrator role. What is the cause?",["The key has automatic rotation turned off","The administrator's session lacks MFA","KMS does not accept IAM policies for customer managed keys, only for AWS managed keys","The key policy does not allow the account, so IAM policies cannot grant access"],3,"Key policies are the main control for KMS keys; IAM policies only work when the key policy allows the account (the usual 'Enable IAM policies' statement). Rotation and MFA are unrelated here, and KMS does honor IAM policies once the key policy allows them.","Domain 5: KMS key policies",["Rotation settings do not affect who is allowed to use a key.","Nothing in the key policy requires MFA, so its absence is not the cause.","KMS does honor IAM policies for customer managed keys when the key policy allows the account.",null],3],["scs68",0,5,"Data encrypted in us-east-1 must be decrypted in eu-west-1 during a failover without calling back to us-east-1 or re-encrypting. What should be used?",["A KMS multi-Region key with a replica in eu-west-1","Two separate customer managed keys with the same alias","A CloudHSM cluster in each Region","SSE-C with the same client key in both Regions"],0,"Multi-Region keys share the same key ID and key material across Regions, so ciphertext from one Region can be decrypted by the replica in another. Keys with the same alias are still different keys, CloudHSM clusters do not share keys this way by default, and SSE-C is S3-only and puts key handling on the client.","Domain 5: multi-Region keys",[null,"Keys with the same alias are still different keys with different material, so ciphertext would not decrypt.","Separate CloudHSM clusters do not automatically share keys and would require a new integration.","SSE-C applies only to S3 and puts key handling on the client, which is not what is asked."],2],["scs69",0,5,"A regulator requires encryption keys to be kept in single-tenant hardware security modules under the company's exclusive control, while AWS services still use KMS for encryption. What should be used?",["AWS managed keys","KMS keys with automatic rotation","A KMS custom key store backed by an AWS CloudHSM cluster","SSE-S3 with S3 Bucket Keys"],2,"A CloudHSM key store keeps key material in a single-tenant CloudHSM cluster that you control while services still call KMS. AWS managed keys and ordinary KMS keys use multi-tenant KMS HSMs, and SSE-S3 keys are managed by S3.","Domain 5: CloudHSM key stores",["AWS managed keys are controlled by AWS and live in shared KMS HSMs, not single-tenant hardware you control.","Rotation changes key material on a schedule but does not change where or who controls the HSMs.",null,"SSE-S3 keys are fully managed by S3, giving the company no control over the hardware."],2],["scs70",0,5,"Automatic rotation is turned on for a customer managed KMS key. What happens to data encrypted before the rotation?",["It must be re-encrypted within 30 days","It can still be decrypted, because KMS keeps older key material for the same key ID","It becomes unreadable until the previous key material is restored by an administrator","It is re-encrypted automatically by KMS"],1,"Rotation adds new key material for new encryptions while keeping older material so existing ciphertext still decrypts, and the key ID and ARN stay the same. Nothing needs re-encrypting, and KMS does not re-encrypt your data.","Domain 5: key rotation",["No re-encryption is needed; old ciphertext stays decryptable after rotation.",null,"Older key material is kept automatically with the same key ID, so nothing becomes unreadable.","KMS never re-encrypts your stored data; it only uses new material for new encryptions."],1],["scs71",0,5,"A bucket using SSE-KMS receives millions of object uploads per day, and KMS request costs and throttling are growing. What should be changed?",["Switch the bucket to SSE-C","Turn off default encryption","Use a different KMS key per prefix","Enable S3 Bucket Keys for SSE-KMS"],3,"S3 Bucket Keys create a short-lived bucket-level key from KMS, which greatly reduces the number of KMS calls while keeping SSE-KMS. SSE-C moves key management to the client, turning off encryption weakens protection, and more keys do not reduce requests.","Domain 5: S3 Bucket Keys",["SSE-C moves key management to every client and makes each request carry the key.","Turning off default encryption weakens protection instead of solving request costs.","Using more keys does not reduce the number of KMS requests per object.",null],2],["scs72",0,5,"Financial records in S3 must not be deletable or overwritten by anyone, including the root user, for seven years. What should be configured?",["S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a seven-year retention period","S3 Object Lock in governance mode","A bucket policy that denies s3:DeleteObject","MFA Delete on the bucket"],0,"Compliance mode prevents any user, including root, from deleting a locked version or shortening retention. Governance mode can be bypassed with a permission, and bucket policies and MFA Delete can be changed or satisfied by privileged users.","Domain 5: Object Lock modes",[null,"Governance mode can be bypassed by users with s3:BypassGovernanceRetention.","A bucket policy can be edited or removed by administrators, including the root user.","MFA Delete only adds an MFA step; the root user with MFA can still delete versions."],1],["scs73",0,5,"Test data is protected by S3 Object Lock in governance mode. A cleanup job must delete some locked versions early. What must the job's role have?",["The s3:PutBucketVersioning permission, so the job can suspend versioning on the bucket first","The kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion permission","The s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission, with the bypass header on the request","The root user's MFA device"],2,"Governance mode can be overridden by principals with s3:BypassGovernanceRetention who send the bypass header. Versioning or KMS permissions do not affect retention, and root MFA is unrelated to governance bypass.","Domain 5: Object Lock governance mode",["Versioning cannot be suspended on a bucket with Object Lock, and it would not release locked versions anyway.","KMS key deletion has nothing to do with S3 retention and would make the data unreadable.",null,"Root MFA is used for MFA Delete, not for bypassing governance retention."],3],["scs74",0,5,"An RDS for MySQL database password must change automatically every 30 days, and applications must always read the current value. What should the team use?",["A Parameter Store String parameter updated by hand","AWS Secrets Manager with rotation for the database secret","An environment variable in each application","An S3 object encrypted with SSE-KMS"],1,"Secrets Manager supports scheduled rotation for RDS credentials, and applications fetch the current version at run time. Parameter Store has no built-in rotation, and environment variables or S3 objects need custom update processes.","Domain 5: Secrets Manager rotation",["Manual updates are error-prone, and Parameter Store has no built-in rotation for database passwords.",null,"Environment variables must be changed and redeployed by hand every time the password changes.","An S3 object needs custom code to rotate the password and keep the database in step."],1],["scs75",0,5,"The company must find which of its 3,000 S3 buckets across the organization contain personal data such as names and passport numbers. What should it use?",["Amazon Inspector","AWS Config","Amazon GuardDuty S3 Protection","Amazon Macie with automated sensitive data discovery"],3,"Macie uses managed and custom data identifiers to find sensitive data in S3 and can run automated discovery across an organization. Inspector finds vulnerabilities, Config tracks configuration, and GuardDuty S3 Protection looks for suspicious access, not data content.","Domain 5: Amazon Macie",["Inspector finds software vulnerabilities, not personal data in S3 objects.","Config records bucket configuration but does not examine the data inside objects.","GuardDuty S3 Protection looks for suspicious access patterns, not sensitive data content.",null],1],["scs76",0,5,"An existing EBS volume attached to a production instance is unencrypted. How can it be encrypted?",["Snapshot it, copy the snapshot with encryption, then create a new volume from the copy","Turn on EBS encryption by default for the Region, which encrypts all existing volumes in place","Run ModifyVolume with the encryption flag","Attach a KMS key to the running volume"],0,"Existing volumes cannot be encrypted in place; the supported path is snapshot, encrypted copy, new volume and swap. Encryption by default only affects new volumes, and ModifyVolume and key attachment do not add encryption.","Domain 5: encrypting existing resources",[null,"Encryption by default only applies to volumes created after it is turned on; existing volumes stay unencrypted.","ModifyVolume changes size, type and performance but cannot add encryption.","There is no way to attach a KMS key to an existing unencrypted volume."],2],["scs77",0,5,"A chatbot built on Amazon Bedrock must never return customer phone numbers or card numbers in its answers. What feature helps most?",["A larger model context window","Bedrock model invocation logging","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails with sensitive information filters","A VPC interface endpoint for Bedrock"],2,"Guardrails can detect and block or mask sensitive information such as personally identifiable information in prompts and responses. Invocation logging records activity but does not filter it, a VPC endpoint keeps traffic private, and context size is not a control.","Domain 5: generative AI data protection",["A larger context window changes how much text the model reads, not what it is allowed to output.","Invocation logging records prompts and responses but does not stop sensitive data being returned.",null,"A VPC endpoint keeps traffic private but does not filter what the model says."],2],["scs78",0,6,"An SCP denies all actions outside two approved Regions and is attached to the organization root. An administrator in the management account can still launch instances in a third Region. Why?",["SCPs only apply to IAM users, not roles","SCPs do not restrict users or roles in the management account","The SCP needs an Allow statement for the management account","Region restrictions require a resource control policy"],1,"Service control policies never affect the management account, which is one reason to keep workloads out of it. SCPs do apply to roles, adding an Allow would not change this, and RCPs control resources rather than Region choice.","Domain 6: SCP scope",["SCPs apply to both IAM users and roles in member accounts.",null,"SCPs cannot be made to apply to the management account by adding statements.","Resource control policies restrict access to resources, not which Regions a principal uses."],3],["scs79",0,6,"The company wants to make sure no S3 bucket or KMS key in any member account can be accessed by principals outside the organization, even if a resource policy allows it. What should it use?",["A service control policy with an aws:PrincipalOrgID condition attached to the root","An IAM permissions boundary on each role","AWS Config rule s3-bucket-public-read-prohibited","A resource control policy that denies access unless aws:PrincipalOrgID matches"],3,"Resource control policies set the maximum permissions on resources in member accounts, whoever calls them, so they can block outside principals centrally. SCPs only restrict principals inside the organization, boundaries apply to identities, and a Config rule only reports public buckets.","Domain 6: resource control policies",["SCPs only restrict principals in your own organization; they cannot limit what outside principals do to your resources.","Permissions boundaries apply to your own identities, not to external callers.","This Config rule only reports public-read buckets and covers no other resource types or cross-account grants.",null],3],["scs80",0,6,"The security team wants every account in an OU to require IMDSv2 for new instances and block public sharing of AMIs, enforced by the service itself even when an API call does not specify it. What should it use?",["An AWS Organizations declarative policy for EC2","An SCP denying ec2:RunInstances","A tag policy for EC2 instances","A CloudFormation Guard rule"],0,"Declarative policies set and enforce a baseline configuration for services such as EC2 across the organization, including IMDS defaults and blocking public AMI and snapshot sharing. SCPs can only allow or deny API calls, tag policies govern tags, and Guard only checks templates.","Domain 6: declarative policies",[null,"An SCP can deny API calls but cannot set service defaults or block AMI sharing when calls do not specify it.","Tag policies standardize tags and do not control instance metadata or AMI sharing.","Guard checks templates before deployment; it does not enforce settings on instances launched another way."],3],["scs81",0,6,"A growing company wants a ready landing zone with separate log archive and audit accounts, organization-wide logging, a way to create new accounts with guardrails, and detective controls. What should it use?",["AWS Service Catalog alone","AWS Resource Access Manager","AWS Control Tower","AWS Trusted Advisor"],2,"Control Tower sets up a landing zone with shared log archive and audit accounts, Account Factory and preventive, detective and proactive controls. Service Catalog, RAM and Trusted Advisor each cover only small parts of this.","Domain 6: AWS Control Tower",["Service Catalog offers approved products but does not create a landing zone, shared accounts or controls.","RAM shares resources between accounts; it does not build an account structure.",null,"Trusted Advisor gives recommendations and does not set up accounts or guardrails."],1],["scs82",0,6,"Why do AWS best practices recommend making a security tooling account the delegated administrator for GuardDuty and Security Hub instead of using the management account?",["The management account cannot enable GuardDuty","It keeps day-to-day security work out of the highly privileged management account","Delegated administrators receive a discount","Only a delegated administrator account can see findings that come from other Regions"],1,"The management account is very powerful and not restricted by SCPs, so its use should be minimal; a delegated administrator gives the security team full service control from a separate account. There is no discount, and the management account can technically enable the services.","Domain 6: delegated administration",["The management account can enable these services; the point is to avoid working in it.",null,"There is no discount for using a delegated administrator.","Cross-Region views come from aggregation features, not from being a delegated administrator."],2],["scs83",0,6,"A baseline stack with an IAM role and Config rules must be deployed to every account in an OU, including accounts added later, without manual steps. What should be used?",["A single CloudFormation stack in the management account that creates the resources everywhere","AWS Systems Manager Run Command","A Lambda function run each night","CloudFormation StackSets with service-managed permissions and automatic deployment"],3,"Service-managed StackSets integrate with Organizations and can deploy automatically to new accounts that join the target OU. A single stack only exists in one account, and Run Command or scheduled Lambda code would need custom logic.","Domain 6: StackSets",["A single stack only creates resources in the account and Region where it runs.","Run Command runs scripts on managed instances; it does not deploy account baselines to new accounts.","A nightly function needs custom code and leaves new accounts unprotected until it runs.",null],2],["scs84",0,6,"Developers need to launch a pre-approved, encrypted database stack without having permission to create databases directly. What should the security team offer?",["An AWS Service Catalog product with a launch constraint role","AdministratorAccess for the developers' sandbox","An SCP that allows rds:CreateDBInstance","A shared IAM user for database work"],0,"Service Catalog products are approved templates, and a launch constraint lets the product launch with a specific role, so users do not need the underlying permissions. Admin access and shared users break least privilege, and SCPs cannot grant permissions.","Domain 6: Service Catalog",[null,"AdministratorAccess gives far more permission than needed and removes guardrails.","SCPs never grant permissions, so this would not let developers launch anything.","A shared user breaks accountability and is a long-term credential to protect."],2],["scs85",0,6,"A platform team wants every CloudFormation template checked in the CI pipeline for rules such as 'S3 buckets must block public access' before anything is deployed. What should it use?",["AWS Trusted Advisor","CloudTrail Insights","AWS CloudFormation Guard rules in the pipeline","AWS Artifact"],2,"CloudFormation Guard evaluates templates against policy-as-code rules and can fail a pipeline before deployment; CloudFormation Hooks can enforce similar checks at deploy time. Trusted Advisor and Insights look at running accounts, and Artifact provides compliance reports.","Domain 6: scanning infrastructure as code",["Trusted Advisor checks running accounts, not templates before deployment.","CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity, not template policy violations.",null,"AWS Artifact provides AWS compliance reports; it does not scan templates."],2],["scs86",0,6,"The company wants to prevent anyone from launching EC2 instances unless the request includes a DataClassification tag. What should it use?",["A tag policy that defines the DataClassification key and its allowed values for EC2","An SCP that denies ec2:RunInstances when aws:RequestTag/DataClassification is null","AWS Resource Groups Tag Editor","A cost allocation tag in the billing console"],1,"An SCP (or IAM policy) with a Null condition on aws:RequestTag blocks untagged launches. Tag policies standardize keys and values but do not by themselves stop resources being created without the tag, and Tag Editor and cost allocation tags are for managing and reporting.","Domain 6: requiring tags",["Tag policies standardize tag keys and values but do not stop resources from being created without the tag.",null,"Tag Editor helps find and edit tags; it does not enforce them at creation.","Cost allocation tags are for billing reports and do not block untagged launches."],3],["scs87",0,6,"A company must deploy the same set of AWS Config rules, mapped to an industry framework, to all accounts in the organization and see their compliance as one package. What should it use?",["A Security Hub custom insight","AWS Audit Manager assessment reports","An IAM Access Analyzer archive rule","Organization-wide AWS Config conformance packs"],3,"Conformance packs bundle Config rules and remediation actions, can be deployed across an organization, and report compliance as a unit. Insights group findings, Audit Manager collects evidence, and archive rules hide Access Analyzer findings.","Domain 6: conformance packs",["Custom insights group Security Hub findings; they do not deploy rules to accounts.","Audit Manager collects evidence for audits but does not deploy Config rules as a package.","Archive rules automatically archive Access Analyzer findings; they are not compliance rules.",null],3],["scs88",0,6,"The compliance team wants one view of AWS Config compliance for all accounts and Regions in the organization. What should be set up?",["An AWS Config aggregator in the delegated administrator account","A CloudTrail organization trail","An Amazon Athena query per account","A Security Hub custom action"],0,"A Config aggregator collects configuration and compliance data from multiple accounts and Regions into one view. CloudTrail records API calls, per-account queries do not give a combined view, and custom actions send findings to EventBridge.","Domain 6: Config aggregators",[null,"An organization trail records API calls, not compliance status of resources.","Separate queries per account give no single view and are hard to maintain.","Custom actions send findings to EventBridge; they do not aggregate Config compliance."],1],["scs89",0,6,"An external auditor asks for AWS's own SOC 2 report to review the controls AWS operates. Where does the company get it?",["AWS Audit Manager","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Artifact","AWS Security Hub"],2,"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports such as SOC and PCI reports, and to agreements. Audit Manager collects evidence about your own use of AWS, while Trusted Advisor and Security Hub check your configuration.","Domain 6: AWS Artifact",["Audit Manager gathers evidence about your own AWS use, not AWS's own audit reports.","Trusted Advisor checks your account against best practices and does not provide audit reports.",null,"Security Hub checks your own configuration against standards; it does not provide AWS's SOC reports."],1],["scs90",0,6,"A company runs PostgreSQL on Amazon RDS. Under the shared responsibility model, which task is the company's job?",["Patching the operating system of the database host that runs the PostgreSQL engine","Controlling who can reach the database through security groups and IAM","Replacing failed storage hardware","Securing the data center where RDS runs"],1,"With a managed service, AWS handles the host OS, hardware and facilities, while the customer controls network access, database users, encryption choices and data. The other three are AWS's responsibilities.","Domain 6: shared responsibility",["AWS patches the host operating system for RDS; customers cannot even access it.",null,"Replacing failed hardware is part of AWS's responsibility for the infrastructure.","Physical data center security is always AWS's responsibility."],2],["x1",0,1,"A company runs containers on Amazon EKS and EC2 instances. The security team wants alerts when a process inside a workload does something suspicious, such as running a crypto miner or opening a reverse shell. Which capability should it enable?",["VPC Flow Logs with a custom format that includes the pkt-srcaddr field","Amazon Inspector scanning of the container images in Amazon ECR","AWS Config recording of the EKS cluster and node group configuration","GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring with the managed security agent"],3,"GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring uses an agent to watch operating system level events such as process execution and network connections inside EC2, EKS and ECS workloads. Inspector finds known vulnerabilities in images but does not watch what running processes do.","Domain 1: GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring",["Flow logs record connection metadata only and cannot see which process inside a container made a connection.","Inspector reports known vulnerabilities in images and packages; it does not observe runtime behavior.","Config records resource configuration changes, not processes executing inside workloads.",null],2],["x2",0,1,"Customers upload files to an S3 bucket through a web form. The security team must check every new object for malware before downstream processing, without building or running its own scanning servers. What should it use?",["GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 on the upload bucket","Amazon Macie automated sensitive data discovery on the bucket","S3 server access logging analyzed daily with Athena queries","An S3 Lifecycle rule that moves new objects to Glacier after one day"],0,"GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 scans newly uploaded objects and can tag them with the result so processing only picks up clean files. Macie looks for sensitive data such as personal information, not malware.","Domain 1: Malware detection",[null,"Macie classifies sensitive data like personal information; it does not detect malicious files.","Access logs show who requested objects, not whether the object content is malicious.","Moving objects to an archive tier changes storage cost, not whether malware is detected."],2],["x3",0,1,"The security team wants every account to be checked continuously against a set of AWS-recommended security controls, such as whether S3 Block Public Access and EBS default encryption are turned on, with a score per account. What should it enable?",["The AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard in Security Hub","Amazon GuardDuty with all protection plans enabled","CloudTrail Insights on the organization trail","Amazon Detective with a behavior graph enabled for every member account in the organization"],0,"Security Hub runs automated checks for the controls in the Foundational Security Best Practices standard and reports a security score. GuardDuty detects threats from activity, not configuration drift against best practices.","Domain 1: Security Hub standards",[null,"GuardDuty detects malicious or unusual activity; it does not score resources against configuration best practices.","CloudTrail Insights flags unusual API call volumes, not missing security settings.","Detective helps investigate findings after the fact and does not run configuration checks."],1],["x4",0,1,"Company policy says every EBS volume must be encrypted. The security team needs a continuously updated list of volumes that break this rule, with a history of when each became noncompliant. What is the simplest option?",["A weekly Amazon Inspector assessment of every EC2 instance","A CloudWatch metric filter on the VPC Flow Logs log group","The AWS Config managed rule for encrypted EBS volumes","A GuardDuty suppression rule scoped to unencrypted volumes"],2,"AWS Config managed rules evaluate resources whenever their configuration changes and keep a compliance timeline for each one. Inspector looks for software vulnerabilities and network exposure, not volume encryption settings.","Domain 1: AWS Config rules",["Inspector reports software vulnerabilities and exposure, not whether volumes are encrypted.","Flow logs contain network traffic records and say nothing about volume encryption.",null,"Suppression rules hide GuardDuty findings; GuardDuty does not evaluate volume encryption."],1],["x5",0,1,"Private instances reach the internet through a NAT gateway. In the default VPC Flow Logs for the NAT gateway's interface, analysts only see the NAT gateway's own address as the source of outbound flows. How can they identify which instance started each connection?",["Enable Route 53 Resolver query logging on the VPC","Switch the flow logs destination from CloudWatch Logs to S3","Turn on NAT gateway access logging in the VPC console","Use a custom flow log format that includes the pkt-srcaddr field"],3,"The pkt-srcaddr field records the original packet source address for traffic that passes through an intermediate interface such as a NAT gateway. Resolver query logs show DNS lookups, which do not prove which instance made each connection.","Domain 1: VPC Flow Logs fields",["DNS query logs show which names were resolved, not the source of each network flow.","Changing the destination does not add fields; the records contain the same data either way.","NAT gateways have no separate access logging feature; flow logs are the traffic record.",null],3],["x6",0,1,"An attacker with stolen credentials could launch hundreds of instances in a short burst. The team wants CloudTrail to flag write API activity that is far above the account's normal baseline, without writing thresholds itself. What should it enable?",["CloudTrail log file integrity validation","S3 Event Notifications on the log bucket","CloudTrail Insights events on the trail","AWS Config advanced queries over the account"],2,"CloudTrail Insights learns normal API call rates and error rates and records an Insights event when activity deviates sharply. Log file integrity validation proves logs were not changed, but does not analyze activity.","Domain 1: CloudTrail Insights",["Integrity validation detects tampering with log files; it does not look for unusual activity.","Event notifications fire when log files arrive, not when API activity is unusual.",null,"Advanced queries report current resource configuration and do not baseline API call rates."],2],["x7",0,1,"AWS sometimes detects that an IAM access key has been published on a public code site and sends a notice. The team wants these notices to start an automated response within minutes. What should it build?",["A daily Trusted Advisor report emailed to the security team for manual review","A CloudWatch alarm on the account's EstimatedCharges metric","An EventBridge rule on AWS Health risk events that starts the response","An IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer for the account"],2,"AWS Health publishes exposed-credential notices as events that EventBridge can match and route to automation, such as a Lambda function or Step Functions workflow. A daily report would add hours of delay while the key is usable.","Domain 1: AWS Health events",["A daily report leaves the exposed key usable for hours before anyone acts.","A billing alarm might fire only after an attacker has already run up large costs.",null,"Unused access analysis finds idle permissions; it does not report keys exposed publicly."],2],["x8",0,1,"Users report that legitimate requests to a web application are being blocked. The application sits behind an Application Load Balancer with an AWS WAF web ACL. What gives the team a full record of which WAF rule matched each request?",["Enable VPC Flow Logs on the load balancer's subnets","Enable CloudTrail data events for the load balancer","Increase the load balancer's idle timeout setting","Turn on AWS WAF logging for the web ACL"],3,"WAF logs record every inspected request with the terminating rule and action. CloudTrail records API calls that manage WAF and the load balancer, not individual web requests evaluated by the rules.","Domain 1: AWS WAF logging",["Flow logs show IP connections, not HTTP request details or which WAF rule matched.","CloudTrail records management API calls, not each web request the web ACL inspects.","The idle timeout controls connection lifetime and has nothing to do with rule matching.",null],1],["x9",0,1,"An internal assistant built on Amazon Bedrock may be targeted by prompt injection. Investigators want to review the exact prompts users sent and the responses the model returned. What should be enabled?",["CloudTrail management events for the Bedrock service","VPC Flow Logs on the interface endpoint for Bedrock","Amazon Macie discovery jobs on the Bedrock console","Bedrock model invocation logging to CloudWatch Logs or S3"],3,"Model invocation logging captures request and response content for Bedrock model calls. CloudTrail management events show who called which API, but not the full prompt and response text.","Domain 1: Generative AI logging",["CloudTrail shows who invoked the API and when, not the full text of prompts and responses.","Flow logs record connection metadata, and the traffic is encrypted, so no prompt content is visible.","Macie scans S3 data for sensitive information; it does not capture model traffic.",null],2],["x10",0,1,"An attacker who gains administrator access may call StopLogging on the organization trail to hide activity. The team wants to be paged within minutes if that happens in any account. Which design works?",["A CloudTrail Lake query scheduled to run at the end of each month and emailed to the security operations team","An S3 Lifecycle rule on the log archive bucket","An EventBridge rule matching the StopLogging API call, sending to an SNS topic","A Config rule that runs every 24 hours to check that logging is on"],2,"CloudTrail management events are delivered to EventBridge in near real time, so a rule on StopLogging (and DeleteTrail) can notify responders quickly. A daily Config check would still detect it, but hours later.","Domain 1: Alerting on logging changes",["A monthly query could find the call weeks after the attacker finished.","Lifecycle rules manage object storage classes and expiry; they do not alert on API calls.",null,"A 24-hour periodic check leaves a long window before anyone learns logging stopped."],3],["x11",0,2,"A Lambda function was modified to send data to an unknown host. The responder must stop the function from running immediately while keeping its code and configuration for analysis. What should be done FIRST?",["Delete the function and redeploy it later from source control","Increase the function timeout so each invocation can be traced","Set the function's reserved concurrency to zero","Publish a new version of the function and point an alias to it"],2,"Reserved concurrency of zero throttles every invocation while leaving the code, versions and configuration intact as evidence. Deleting the function stops it too, but destroys the modified code the investigation needs.","Domain 2: Containing serverless workloads",["Deleting the function removes the tampered code and configuration that must be analyzed.","A longer timeout lets the malicious code run even longer and contains nothing.",null,"A new version and alias do not stop callers that invoke the tampered version directly."],3],["x12",0,2,"Responders must share an EBS snapshot of a compromised instance with a separate forensics account. The volume was encrypted with the AWS managed key aws/ebs, and the share fails. What should they do?",["Edit the aws/ebs key policy to let the forensics account use the key, then share the snapshot","Make the snapshot public for a short time and then make it private again","Disable encryption on the snapshot before sharing it","Copy the snapshot using a customer managed key shared with the forensics account"],3,"Snapshots encrypted with an AWS managed key cannot be shared because that key's policy cannot be changed. Copying with a customer managed key whose policy allows the forensics account makes the share work. Encrypted snapshots cannot be made public, and encryption cannot be removed.","Domain 2: Forensic evidence sharing",["AWS managed key policies cannot be edited, so the key cannot be shared with another account.","Encrypted snapshots cannot be made public, and exposing evidence publicly is never acceptable.","Encryption cannot be removed from an existing snapshot.",null],3],["x13",0,2,"Someone with stolen credentials deleted thousands of objects from a bucket that has versioning enabled. Listing the bucket now shows no objects. What is the quickest way to restore them?",["Restore the bucket from the most recent CloudTrail log files","Remove the delete markers so earlier versions become current again","Suspend versioning so the previous objects reappear","Request that AWS Support undelete the bucket contents"],1,"In a versioned bucket, a simple DELETE adds a delete marker and keeps the prior versions; removing the markers makes those versions current again. Suspending versioning does not bring back objects and only changes how future writes are handled.","Domain 2: Recovering S3 data",["CloudTrail records API calls; it does not contain object data to restore from.",null,"Suspending versioning affects future writes and does not remove existing delete markers.","AWS Support cannot restore customer objects; the versions in the bucket are the recovery path."],2],["x14",0,2,"A compromised EC2 instance belongs to an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. Before isolating it, what should the responder do so that evidence is not lost?",["Terminate it right away so the Auto Scaling group launches a clean replacement instance","Detach it from the Auto Scaling group and deregister it from the target group","Reboot it to clear the attacker's running processes","Stop the instance and change its instance type to a larger size"],1,"If the instance fails health checks after isolation, the Auto Scaling group may terminate it and destroy memory and disk evidence; detaching it prevents that, and deregistering stops user traffic from reaching it. Terminating or rebooting destroys volatile evidence.","Domain 2: EC2 containment",["Terminating the instance destroys memory and possibly disk evidence before capture.",null,"A reboot wipes memory, which is often the most valuable evidence.","Stopping the instance also loses memory contents, and resizing adds no containment."],3],["x15",0,2,"The company wants a critical security finding to automatically engage the on-call responder, escalate if nobody acknowledges within 10 minutes, open a chat channel and track the timeline. Which AWS service is designed for this?",["AWS Trusted Advisor with weekly email notifications sent to the on-call team","AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager with response plans","Amazon Detective behavior graphs","AWS Config conformance packs"],1,"Incident Manager response plans define contacts, escalation plans, chat channels and runbooks, and keep an incident timeline. Detective helps investigate a finding but does not page or escalate to people.","Domain 2: Incident response coordination",["Trusted Advisor gives best practice checks; it does not page or escalate to responders.",null,"Detective supports investigation but has no on-call engagement or escalation.","Conformance packs group Config rules for compliance and do not manage incidents."],2],["x16",0,2,"During an incident, CloudTrail shows that the attacker called ScheduleKeyDeletion on the customer managed KMS key that protects the main database. The waiting period has not ended. What should the responder do?",["Create a new key with the same alias and re-encrypt the database later","Nothing, because data encrypted with a scheduled key stays readable after deletion","Import new key material into the key before the deletion date","Cancel the key deletion, then re-enable the key"],3,"During the 7 to 30 day waiting period, CancelKeyDeletion restores the key, which is left disabled and must be enabled again. Once a key is deleted, data encrypted under it can no longer be decrypted.","Domain 2: Recovering KMS keys",["A new key cannot decrypt data encrypted under the old key, so the database would become unreadable.","After deletion, data encrypted under the key cannot be decrypted at all.","Key material can only be imported into keys created for external material, and this does not stop deletion.",null],2],["x17",0,2,"An instance under investigation must not be terminated by mistake by another engineer or by automation. What should the responder set on the instance?",["Enable termination protection and set shutdown behavior to stop","Attach a new Elastic IP address to the instance","Add the instance to a placement group","Enable detailed CloudWatch monitoring"],0,"Termination protection blocks TerminateInstances calls until it is turned off, and a stop shutdown behavior keeps the instance if someone shuts it down from inside the OS. Detailed monitoring only adds metric frequency.","Domain 2: Preserving evidence",[null,"An Elastic IP changes addressing and adds exposure; it does not prevent termination.","Placement groups control physical placement for performance, not termination.","Detailed monitoring increases metric frequency and does not protect the instance."],1],["x18",0,2,"Analysts reviewing findings in Security Hub want a button that sends a selected finding to the company's ticketing system and response automation. What should they configure?",["A GuardDuty trusted IP list that contains the ticketing system's public IP address and API endpoint","An AWS Config aggregator in the delegated administrator account","A Security Hub custom action with an EventBridge rule that targets the automation","A Security Hub custom insight grouped by resource type"],2,"A custom action sends the selected findings to EventBridge as a Security Hub event, where a rule can invoke Lambda or other targets. A custom insight only groups and filters findings for viewing.","Domain 2: Response automation",["Trusted IP lists stop GuardDuty findings for listed addresses; they do not send findings anywhere.","A Config aggregator collects compliance data; it does not route selected findings to tools.",null,"Custom insights filter and group findings for display and cannot trigger automation."],2],["x19",0,3,"Web servers in an Auto Scaling group must be the only sources allowed to reach a database on port 5432. Web server IP addresses change often. How should the database security group rule be written?",["Allow port 5432 from the VPC's entire CIDR block","Allow port 5432 with the web servers' security group as the source","Allow port 5432 from 0.0.0.0/0 and rely on the database password for protection","Allow all ports from the web tier subnet's CIDR range"],1,"Referencing a security group as the source allows traffic from any instance that uses that group, whatever its IP address. Allowing the whole VPC CIDR would let every workload in the VPC reach the database.","Domain 3: Security groups",["The whole VPC range lets unrelated workloads reach the database, which breaks least privilege.",null,"Opening the port to any address exposes the database and makes the password the only barrier.","Allowing all ports opens far more than the database needs and includes other hosts in the subnet."],1],["x20",0,3,"A network ACL on a public subnet allows inbound TCP 443 from anywhere and has an outbound rule allowing only TCP 443. Clients can connect but receive no responses. What is the most likely fix?",["Add an inbound rule allowing ICMP from anywhere","Allow outbound TCP to the ephemeral port range 1024-65535","Allow outbound TCP 443 to the VPC CIDR block only","Change the subnet's route table to use a NAT gateway"],1,"Network ACLs are stateless, so responses to clients go back to the client's ephemeral port and need their own outbound rule. ICMP is not what carries HTTPS responses, so allowing it does not fix the problem.","Domain 3: Network ACLs",["ICMP is not used to return HTTPS responses, so the replies would still be dropped.",null,"Responses go to internet clients, not the VPC range, and to their ephemeral ports, not 443.","A public subnet serving clients needs its internet gateway route; a NAT gateway is for outbound-only private traffic."],2],["x21",0,3,"A company with 50 accounts must make sure every Application Load Balancer and CloudFront distribution, including ones created next month, has a baseline set of AWS WAF rules applied. What should it use?",["A CloudFormation template that each team deploys manually","An AWS Config rule that only reports resources without a web ACL","AWS Firewall Manager WAF policies scoped to the organization","Shield Standard, which applies WAF rules to all resources automatically"],2,"Firewall Manager applies WAF policies across accounts in an organization and automatically protects new in-scope resources. A Config rule alone would report gaps but not fix them.","Domain 3: AWS Firewall Manager",["Manual deployment relies on every team remembering, so new resources can be missed.","A reporting rule shows missing web ACLs but does not attach any rules.",null,"Shield Standard gives network and transport layer DDoS protection and does not apply WAF rules."],2],["x22",0,3,"A public web application keeps receiving requests from IP addresses known for bots and reconnaissance. The team wants to block them with minimal effort and have the list kept current by AWS. What should it add to its web ACL?",["The AWS managed Amazon IP reputation list rule group","A custom IP set that the team updates each month by hand","A security group rule that denies the addresses","A network ACL with one deny rule per address"],0,"The Amazon IP reputation list managed rule group is maintained by AWS threat intelligence and blocks addresses associated with bots and other threats. Security groups have no deny rules, so they cannot block specific addresses.","Domain 3: AWS WAF managed rules",[null,"A hand-maintained list falls out of date and takes ongoing effort.","Security groups only support allow rules, so they cannot deny addresses.","Network ACLs have a small rule limit and would need constant manual updates."],1],["x23",0,3,"Applications in private subnets with no NAT gateway must read secrets from AWS Secrets Manager, and the traffic must stay on the AWS network. What should be created?",["A gateway VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager","An internet gateway with a route from the private subnets","A VPC peering connection from each private VPC to the Secrets Manager service VPC","An interface VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager with private DNS enabled"],3,"Secrets Manager is reached privately through an interface endpoint (AWS PrivateLink), and private DNS lets the default service name resolve to the endpoint. Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB.","Domain 3: VPC endpoints",["Gateway endpoints are available only for S3 and DynamoDB, not Secrets Manager.","An internet gateway route would send traffic over public endpoints and expose the subnets.","AWS services are not reached by peering with a service VPC; PrivateLink endpoints are used.",null],2],["x24",0,3,"Administrators must use their standard SSH clients to reach Linux instances in private subnets. The instances have no public IP addresses, the team does not want to run a bastion host, and access must be authorized with IAM. What fits BEST?",["A public Network Load Balancer forwarding port 22 to the instances","Elastic IP addresses on each instance with SSH limited to office IPs","A VPC peering connection between the VPC and the administrators' home networks","An EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint in the VPC"],3,"An EC2 Instance Connect Endpoint lets IAM-authorized users open SSH or RDP connections to private instances without public IPs, bastions or internet gateways. Adding Elastic IPs exposes each instance directly to the internet, even if filtered.","Domain 3: Private administrative access",["Publishing port 22 through a public load balancer exposes SSH to the internet.","Public addresses on every instance widen the attack surface and ignore the no-public-IP rule.","Peering connects VPCs to other VPCs, not to home networks.",null],3],["x25",0,3,"Operations must apply approved operating system security patches to 400 EC2 instances every month during a defined window, and report which instances are missing patches. What should be used?",["Systems Manager Patch Manager with patch baselines and a maintenance window","Amazon Inspector alone, which installs any missing operating system patches automatically","AWS Config conformance packs set to remediate every instance","An AMI rebuild from the Marketplace every six months"],0,"Patch Manager uses patch baselines to decide which patches are approved, installs them during maintenance windows and reports compliance. Inspector identifies missing patches but does not install them.","Domain 3: Patch management",[null,"Inspector identifies vulnerabilities and missing patches but does not install anything.","Conformance packs evaluate configuration compliance; they are not a patch installation tool.","Rebuilding every six months leaves instances unpatched for months, which misses the monthly requirement."],1],["x26",0,3,"Security policy requires all traffic between VPCs and the internet to pass through a third-party next-generation firewall appliance, deployed as a scalable fleet. Which AWS service is designed to insert such appliances transparently?",["Application Load Balancer with path-based routing rules","Route 53 Resolver outbound endpoints","A NAT gateway in each Availability Zone","Gateway Load Balancer with Gateway Load Balancer endpoints"],3,"Gateway Load Balancer distributes traffic to a fleet of virtual appliances using GENEVE while keeping the original packets intact, and its endpoints are used as route targets. An Application Load Balancer works at layer 7 for HTTP and cannot route all traffic through appliances.","Domain 3: Inline inspection",["An Application Load Balancer handles HTTP requests to targets and cannot act as a transparent inline hop.","Resolver outbound endpoints forward DNS queries, not general network traffic.","NAT gateways translate addresses and do no deep inspection through third-party appliances.",null],2],["x27",0,3,"Because of export rules, a media company must stop users in three specific countries from viewing content served through CloudFront. What is the simplest control?",["CloudFront geographic restrictions with a block list of those countries","Security groups on the origin servers","Signed URLs issued to every user","An S3 bucket policy with the aws:SourceIp condition"],0,"CloudFront geographic restrictions block requests based on the viewer's country at the edge. A bucket policy on the origin sees CloudFront's addresses, not viewers' IP addresses, so it cannot filter by viewer country.","Domain 3: Edge security",[null,"Origin security groups see CloudFront's addresses, not the viewers' locations.","Signed URLs control who has a valid link, not which country a viewer is in.","The origin sees CloudFront as the caller, so viewer IP conditions in the bucket policy do not work."],1],["x28",0,3,"Teams launch EC2 instances from AMIs they build by hand, and many are missing hardening settings. The security team wants a repeatable pipeline that builds hardened AMIs, tests them and publishes new versions on a schedule. What should it use?",["AWS Backup plans that copy existing AMIs across Regions","AWS Service Catalog portfolios listing the current hand-built AMIs","Launch templates that point to the latest Marketplace AMI","EC2 Image Builder with hardening components and test steps"],3,"EC2 Image Builder automates building, hardening, testing and distributing images on a schedule. Service Catalog can offer approved products to teams but does not build or harden the images.","Domain 3: Hardened images",["Backup copies existing images as they are and adds no hardening or testing.","Service Catalog can distribute approved items but does not build or harden images.","A Marketplace AMI is not hardened to company policy, and 'latest' is not tested by the team.",null],2],["x29",0,3,"Production and development VPCs are attached to the same transit gateway, which uses its default route table. A new requirement says development VPCs must never reach production, while both still reach a shared services VPC. What should be changed?",["Add security group rules on every production instance that deny all traffic from the development CIDR ranges","Separate transit gateway route tables per environment, propagating only allowed attachments","Replace the transit gateway with a single large VPC for all environments","Enable DNS support on the transit gateway attachments"],1,"Transit gateway route tables control which attachments can route to each other, so associating each environment with its own table and propagating only shared services enforces segmentation. Security groups cannot contain deny rules.","Domain 3: Network segmentation",["Security groups only allow traffic and cannot express deny rules.",null,"Merging all environments into one VPC removes the separation instead of enforcing it.","DNS support affects name resolution and does not restrict routing between VPCs."],3],["x30",0,4,"An IAM user has the AdministratorAccess managed policy attached. The user also has a permissions boundary that allows only s3:* and cloudwatch:*. No other policies apply. What happens when the user calls ec2:RunInstances?",["It is denied, because the boundary does not allow it","It is allowed, because AdministratorAccess grants every action","It is allowed, because boundaries apply only to roles","It is denied only if an SCP also denies EC2"],0,"Effective permissions are the intersection of the identity policy and the permissions boundary, so an action outside the boundary is implicitly denied. The tempting answer ignores that a boundary sets a maximum even when the identity policy allows everything.","Domain 4: Permissions boundaries",[null,"The identity policy allows it, but the boundary caps permissions and does not include EC2.","Permissions boundaries can be set on both IAM users and IAM roles.","No SCP is needed to deny it; the boundary alone blocks the call."],3],["x31",0,4,"An application assumes a role that allows s3:GetObject and s3:PutObject on a bucket. When calling AssumeRole, it passes a session policy that allows only s3:GetObject. What can the resulting session do?",["Read and write, because the role's policy takes precedence","Read and write, because session policies only add permissions","Only read objects, because the session policy further limits the role","Nothing, because a session policy replaces all of the role's permissions with an empty set"],2,"A session's effective permissions are the intersection of the role's identity policies and the session policy, so it can only read. Session policies never grant permissions beyond the role; they can only narrow them.","Domain 4: Session policies",["The role's policy does not override the session policy; both must allow the action.","Session policies can only restrict permissions, never add to them.",null,"The session keeps the permissions that both the role and the session policy allow."],3],["x32",0,4,"A bucket policy contains an explicit Deny for s3:DeleteObject for all principals. A user's IAM policy explicitly allows s3:DeleteObject on that bucket. What happens when the user tries to delete an object?",["The request is allowed, because identity policies are evaluated first","The request is denied, because an explicit deny overrides any allow","The request is allowed, because the user owns the IAM policy","The result depends on which policy was changed most recently"],1,"In IAM policy evaluation, an explicit deny in any applicable policy always wins over allows. The order in which policies were written or evaluated does not change this.","Domain 4: Policy evaluation logic",["Evaluation order does not let an allow beat an explicit deny.",null,"Ownership of a policy does not change evaluation; the explicit deny still wins.","Policy modification time plays no part in authorization decisions."],1],["x33",0,4,"A bucket must be usable only by one application role, even though several administrators in the account have s3:* in their IAM policies. Which bucket policy statement BEST achieves this?",["Deny s3:* when aws:PrincipalArn does not equal the application role's ARN","Allow s3:* for the application role in the bucket policy and add no other statements","Deny s3:* for the root user of the account","Allow s3:* when aws:SecureTransport is true"],0,"A Deny with a condition on aws:PrincipalArn blocks every other principal, including administrators whose identity policies allow S3. An Allow alone does nothing to stop same-account principals who already have identity-based permissions.","Domain 4: Restricting resource access",[null,"In the same account, administrators' identity policies still grant access without an explicit deny.","Denying only the root user leaves every administrator role and user with access.","Requiring TLS protects data in transit but does not limit which principals can use the bucket."],3],["x34",0,4,"A script assumes role A, then uses those credentials to assume role B. The script requests a 4-hour session for role B, but the call fails. Role B's maximum session duration is 12 hours. Why?",["Role B's trust policy must list the script's IP address","Sessions can never be longer than the default of 15 minutes","The script must use long-term access keys to assume role B","Role chaining limits sessions to a maximum of one hour"],3,"When a role session assumes another role (role chaining), the new session is limited to one hour regardless of the role's maximum session duration setting. The 15-minute value is the minimum duration, not the limit.","Domain 4: Role chaining",["Trust policies do not require source IP addresses to allow longer sessions.","Fifteen minutes is the minimum session duration, not the maximum.","Using long-term keys would weaken security and is not required to assume a role.",null],3],["x35",0,4,"Developers submit IAM policies through a CI pipeline. The security team wants the pipeline to fail if a change would grant new access compared with the approved version, or if a policy allows a listed sensitive action. What should it use?",["IAM Access Analyzer custom policy checks in the pipeline","The IAM credential report generated on each commit","CloudTrail Lake queries run after deployment","AWS Trusted Advisor security checks"],0,"Access Analyzer custom policy checks, such as check-no-new-access and check-access-not-granted, compare policies before deployment and return pass or fail results. Post-deployment queries only find problems after the access already exists.","Domain 4: Policy validation",[null,"The credential report lists users and credential status; it does not analyze policy changes.","Queries after deployment detect problems only once the risky access is already live.","Trusted Advisor gives account-level checks and does not review policies in a pipeline."],2],["x36",0,4,"A consumer app uses an Amazon Cognito user pool. The team wants to detect sign-ins with credentials found in public breach lists and require extra verification when a sign-in looks risky. What should it enable?",["Cognito threat protection with adaptive authentication","A longer password minimum length in the user pool's password policy","GuardDuty EKS Protection on the account","An identity pool with unauthenticated access enabled"],0,"Cognito threat protection (formerly advanced security features) scores sign-in risk, can require MFA or block risky attempts, and detects compromised credentials. A longer password policy helps new passwords but does not detect reused, already-breached ones.","Domain 4: Amazon Cognito",[null,"A stronger password policy does not detect passwords already exposed in breaches.","EKS Protection monitors Kubernetes audit logs, not Cognito sign-ins.","Unauthenticated identities give guest access and weaken, rather than strengthen, sign-in security."],2],["x37",0,4,"Pods in an Amazon EKS cluster currently use the worker node's instance role, so every pod can reach every AWS resource the node can. What should the team do to give each application only its own permissions?",["Add all application permissions to the node role and separate the applications with Kubernetes namespaces","Use EKS Pod Identity or IAM roles for service accounts to map each service account to its own role","Store an IAM user's access keys in a Kubernetes Secret for each application","Enable IMDSv1 so pods can fetch credentials faster"],1,"EKS Pod Identity and IRSA give each Kubernetes service account its own IAM role, so pods receive only their application's permissions. Namespaces isolate Kubernetes objects but do not split the node role's AWS permissions.","Domain 4: Workload identity on EKS",["Namespaces do not change AWS permissions; every pod could still use the broad node role.",null,"Long-term keys in Secrets are hard to rotate and easy to leak.","IMDSv1 is less secure than IMDSv2 and still hands out the node role to pods."],2],["x38",0,4,"IAM users should be able to change their own passwords and manage their own MFA devices and access keys, but not anyone else's, using one policy attached to a group. What makes this possible?",["A separate inline policy written for each user","The aws:SourceVpc condition key","A service control policy attached to the root","The ${aws:username} policy variable in the resource ARN"],3,"Policy variables such as ${aws:username} are replaced with the caller's user name at evaluation time, so one group policy can limit each user to their own resources. Writing a policy per user works but does not scale.","Domain 4: Policy variables",["Per-user policies work but create many policies to maintain, unlike one variable-based policy.","aws:SourceVpc restricts requests by VPC endpoint origin, not which user's resources are touched.","SCPs set maximum permissions for accounts; they cannot scope access to each user's own credentials.",null],2],["x39",0,4,"A shared bucket should be readable by principals in any account under the organization's Finance OU, including accounts added later, but not by other accounts in the organization. Which condition key should the bucket policy use?",["aws:PrincipalOrgID","aws:SourceAccount","aws:PrincipalOrgPaths","aws:ResourceTag/OU"],2,"aws:PrincipalOrgPaths matches the caller's path in the organization, so a policy can allow any account under a specific OU. aws:PrincipalOrgID would allow every account in the organization, which is too broad here.","Domain 4: Organization condition keys",["The organization ID allows all accounts in the organization, not just the Finance OU.","aws:SourceAccount is used for service principals acting on behalf of an account, not for OU membership.",null,"A resource tag describes the bucket, not which OU the calling principal belongs to."],3],["x40",0,4,"An auditor wants a downloadable list of all IAM users in an account showing whether each has MFA enabled, when passwords were last used and how old each access key is. What should the administrator provide?",["The IAM credential report","The AWS Artifact SOC 2 report","A VPC Flow Logs export","An Amazon Inspector findings export"],0,"The IAM credential report is a CSV listing every user and the status of their passwords, access keys and MFA devices. AWS Artifact provides AWS's own compliance reports, not details about the customer's users.","Domain 4: IAM credential report",[null,"Artifact holds AWS's compliance reports and says nothing about the customer's IAM users.","Flow logs contain network traffic records, not user credential details.","Inspector reports software vulnerabilities, not MFA or key age for IAM users."],1],["x41",0,4,"A developer can call ec2:RunInstances but gets an access denied error when launching an instance with an instance profile that grants S3 access. What permission is most likely missing?",["iam:CreateRole for a new copy of the role","sts:GetCallerIdentity for the developer's session","iam:PassRole on the role in the instance profile","ec2:DescribeInstances for the target subnet"],2,"Attaching a role to a service requires iam:PassRole on that role, which stops users from handing a service more privileges than they have. Creating a new role is unrelated to passing an existing one.","Domain 4: iam:PassRole",["Creating roles is a separate, broader permission and is not needed to attach an existing role.","GetCallerIdentity needs no permissions and is not checked when launching instances.",null,"Describe permissions affect listing resources, not whether a role can be attached at launch."],2],["x42",0,5,"Hundreds of internal microservices need TLS certificates for service-to-service traffic. The certificates must be trusted only inside the company and renewed automatically. What should the team use?",["Public ACM certificates with DNS validation for each internal name","AWS Private Certificate Authority with ACM private certificates","Self-signed certificates generated on each host","Certificates bought from a public certificate authority each year"],1,"AWS Private CA issues certificates for a private hierarchy that only the company's systems trust, and ACM can manage and renew them. Public certificates require publicly resolvable domains and expose internal names in certificate transparency logs.","Domain 5: Private certificates",["Public certificates need publicly validated domains and publish names in transparency logs.",null,"Self-signed certificates on each host are hard to trust, track and rotate at scale.","Buying public certificates yearly adds cost and manual renewal without private trust."],2],["x43",0,5,"A customer managed KMS key protects S3 objects. The team wants the key usable for encryption and decryption only when the request comes through Amazon S3, even for principals whose policies allow kms:Decrypt. Which key policy condition fits?",["kms:KeyOrigin set to AWS_KMS","kms:ViaService set to the S3 service endpoint for the Region","aws:SecureTransport set to true","kms:GrantIsForAWSResource set to false"],1,"kms:ViaService limits use of the key to requests that an AWS service such as S3 makes on the principal's behalf, blocking direct kms:Decrypt calls. SecureTransport only requires TLS and does not limit which path calls the key.","Domain 5: KMS key policy conditions",["KeyOrigin describes where the key material came from, not which service uses the key.",null,"SecureTransport requires TLS but still allows direct calls to KMS from any client.","GrantIsForAWSResource applies to creating grants, not to direct encryption or decryption calls."],3],["x44",0,5,"An application encrypts records with KMS and passes an encryption context containing the tenant ID. A bug later calls Decrypt with a different tenant ID in the context. What happens?",["Decryption fails, because the context must match exactly","Decryption succeeds, because the context is only logged","Decryption succeeds, but returns the data re-encrypted","KMS ignores the context unless the key has rotation enabled"],0,"Encryption context is additional authenticated data bound to the ciphertext, so decryption requires the same context. It is also logged in CloudTrail, but that is not its only role, which makes 'only logged' the tempting wrong answer.","Domain 5: KMS encryption context",[null,"The context is logged, but it is also cryptographically bound, so a mismatch makes decryption fail.","KMS never returns re-encrypted data from a failed Decrypt call.","Encryption context is enforced for every symmetric key, whatever its rotation setting."],3],["x45",0,5,"A company uploads objects to S3 using SSE-C, supplying its own encryption key with each request. What must the company understand about this option?",["S3 keeps a copy of the key in KMS so that it can be recovered later if the company loses it","The key is stored in CloudHSM, which the company must operate","AWS does not store the key, so a lost key means the objects cannot be read","SSE-C encrypts only object metadata, not the object data"],2,"With SSE-C, S3 uses the supplied key to encrypt or decrypt and then discards it, keeping only a salted HMAC to validate future requests. The company must manage the keys itself; S3 cannot recover them.","Domain 5: S3 encryption options",["S3 does not store SSE-C keys in KMS or anywhere else, so there is no recovery copy.","SSE-C does not involve CloudHSM; the customer supplies the key with each request.",null,"SSE-C encrypts the object data using the supplied key."],2],["x46",0,5,"A DynamoDB table stores customer records. Database administrators with full DynamoDB permissions must not be able to read the national ID attribute, while the application still can. What BEST meets this?",["DynamoDB encryption at rest using an AWS owned key, which is the table default","A DynamoDB stream that masks the attribute","Client-side encryption of the attribute before it is stored","Point-in-time recovery for the table"],2,"Client-side, attribute-level encryption means the value is stored encrypted and only callers with access to the KMS key used for that encryption can decrypt it. Encryption at rest is transparent to anyone with table permissions, so administrators would still read the value.","Domain 5: Client-side encryption",["Encryption at rest is transparent, so anyone who can read the table sees the plaintext.","Streams copy item changes elsewhere; they do not hide values stored in the table.",null,"Point-in-time recovery provides backups and does nothing to restrict who reads attributes."],3],["x47",0,5,"An Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance was created without encryption. The security team now requires it to be encrypted with a customer managed KMS key. What is the correct approach?",["Modify the instance and turn on encryption in place","Snapshot it, copy the snapshot with encryption, then restore a new instance","Enable EBS encryption by default in the Region","Add an encryption option to the parameter group and reboot"],1,"RDS encryption can only be set when an instance is created, so the path is an encrypted snapshot copy and a restore to a new instance. EBS default encryption affects new EBS volumes, not existing RDS storage.","Domain 5: RDS encryption",["RDS does not allow turning on encryption for an existing unencrypted instance.",null,"EBS default encryption applies to new EBS volumes and does not change existing RDS instances.","Parameter groups set database engine settings, not storage encryption."],2],["x48",0,5,"An application running in two Regions reads a database credential from Secrets Manager in us-east-1. During a Region outage, the standby Region must still read the current secret. What should be configured?",["An S3 bucket in the standby Region holding a plain-text export of the secret","A Parameter Store String parameter updated manually in each Region","An IAM policy allowing the standby Region to read us-east-1 only","Secrets Manager replication of the secret to the standby Region"],3,"Replica secrets stay in sync with the primary, including rotations, and can be read or promoted in the other Region. Relying on cross-Region reads to us-east-1 fails exactly when that Region is down.","Domain 5: Secrets management",["A plain-text copy in S3 exposes the credential and goes stale after rotation.","Manual String parameters store the secret unencrypted and drift out of sync.","Reading from us-east-1 fails during the very outage the design must survive.",null],2],["x49",0,5,"A healthcare company must process decrypted patient data on EC2 in an environment that has no persistent storage, no interactive access and no external networking, not even for root users on the parent instance. What should it use?",["AWS Nitro Enclaves","Dedicated Hosts","EC2 instance store volumes","A placement group with spread strategy"],0,"Nitro Enclaves create isolated compute environments with no persistent storage, interactive access or external networking, and support attestation with KMS. Dedicated Hosts give single-tenant hardware but the instance's own administrators can still reach the data.","Domain 5: Isolated processing",[null,"Dedicated Hosts isolate hardware from other customers but not from the company's own administrators.","Instance store is temporary storage and gives no isolation from users on the instance.","Spread placement groups affect hardware placement for availability, not data isolation."],3],["x50",0,5,"A company imported its own key material into a KMS key so it can prove it generated the key. Which statement about this key is true?",["KMS rotates the imported key material automatically once a year, the same as for AWS managed keys","KMS keeps a backup copy, so expired material restores itself","Imported keys cannot be used with any AWS service integration","The company must rotate it manually and can set the imported material to expire"],3,"Keys with imported material do not support automatic rotation, and the material can be given an expiration date or deleted, making the key unusable until reimported. They still work with AWS services that integrate with KMS.","Domain 5: Imported key material",["Automatic rotation is not supported for keys with imported key material.","When imported material expires or is deleted, the company must reimport it; KMS does not restore it.","Keys with imported material work with integrated AWS services like any symmetric KMS key.",null],3],["x51",0,5,"Amazon Macie must find S3 objects that contain the company's internal employee ID format, which is not one of Macie's built-in data types. What should the team create?",["A GuardDuty threat list with the ID format","An S3 Inventory report filtered by object size","A Macie custom data identifier using a regular expression","A Config rule that inspects object content"],2,"Custom data identifiers let Macie detect organization-specific sensitive data using a regular expression and optional keywords. Config evaluates resource configuration and does not read object contents.","Domain 5: Amazon Macie",["Threat lists are sets of malicious IPs or domains for GuardDuty, not data patterns.","Inventory reports list objects and metadata; they do not inspect content.",null,"Config rules evaluate resource settings and cannot scan the content of objects."],1],["x52",0,5,"After a ransomware scare, the company requires that backups in AWS Backup cannot be deleted before their retention ends, even by an administrator whose account is compromised. What should it enable?",["S3 Transfer Acceleration on the backup bucket","AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode on the backup vault","A longer default retention in the backup plan only","An IAM permissions boundary on the backup service role"],1,"Vault Lock in compliance mode makes the vault immutable once the grace period ends, so no one can delete recovery points early or change the lock. A longer plan retention can still be edited or bypassed by a compromised administrator.","Domain 5: Backup protection",["Transfer Acceleration speeds uploads and does nothing to stop deletion.",null,"Retention settings alone can be changed or bypassed by an administrator with enough permissions.","A boundary on the service role does not stop a compromised administrator from deleting recovery points."],2],["x53",0,6,"The security team wants to make sure no member account can remove itself from the AWS organization and escape its guardrails. What should it do?",["Enable AWS Config in each member account","Turn on CloudTrail log file integrity validation","Attach an SCP that denies organizations:LeaveOrganization","Tag each account with its owner's name"],2,"An SCP denying organizations:LeaveOrganization prevents member accounts from leaving, so they stay subject to organization policies. Config would record changes but cannot block the API call.","Domain 6: Organizations guardrails",["Config records and evaluates configuration; it cannot prevent an account from leaving.","Integrity validation protects log files and does not stop an account from leaving.",null,"Tags describe accounts but do not enforce anything."],1],["x54",0,6,"An administrator attaches an SCP that allows s3:* to an OU, expecting it to give all users in those accounts S3 access. Users without IAM policies still cannot use S3. Why?",["SCPs take up to 24 hours to apply to the member accounts in an OU after being attached","SCPs only set maximum permissions and never grant access by themselves","SCPs must be attached directly to each IAM user","SCPs apply only to the management account"],1,"An SCP defines the most an account's principals can do; an identity or resource policy must still grant the permission. SCPs do not apply to the management account at all, which is the opposite of the last option.","Domain 6: Service control policies",["SCP changes take effect quickly; a delay does not explain the lack of access.",null,"SCPs attach to the root, OUs or accounts, not to individual IAM users.","SCPs do not affect the management account; they apply to member accounts."],2],["x55",0,6,"In an AWS Control Tower landing zone, the team wants CloudFormation deployments to be blocked before provisioning if they would create an S3 bucket without encryption, rather than finding the bucket afterward. Which type of control is this?",["A detective control","A proactive control","A preventive control implemented only with SCPs","A mandatory control applied to the management account"],1,"Control Tower proactive controls use CloudFormation hooks to check resources before they are provisioned and block noncompliant ones. Detective controls use AWS Config to find noncompliant resources after they exist.","Domain 6: Control Tower controls",["Detective controls report noncompliant resources only after they have been created.",null,"SCP-based preventive controls block API actions but cannot inspect the properties of a CloudFormation resource before provisioning.","Mandatory controls are baseline controls Control Tower applies itself, not a type that checks templates."],3],["x56",0,6,"A security team manages Security Hub from a delegated administrator account. It wants different security standards and controls enabled for production OUs and sandbox OUs, applied automatically to new accounts. What should it use?",["A separate manual setup of standards and controls in each member account","A single Config rule deployed to the management account","Security Hub central configuration policies associated with OUs","Amazon Detective membership invitations"],2,"Central configuration lets the delegated administrator create configuration policies that enable standards and controls and associate them with OUs or accounts, including new ones. Manual setup per account is error-prone and does not cover new accounts.","Domain 6: Security Hub central configuration",["Manual setup per account drifts over time and misses new accounts.","A Config rule in the management account does not configure Security Hub standards across OUs.",null,"Detective invitations manage investigation membership, not Security Hub standards."],2],["x57",0,6,"An internal audit needs evidence mapped to a regulatory framework: configuration snapshots, CloudTrail activity and manual attestations, collected continuously and organized by control. Which service is designed for this?",["AWS Audit Manager","AWS Artifact","Amazon Inspector","AWS Trusted Advisor"],0,"Audit Manager collects evidence automatically from sources like Config and CloudTrail, supports manual evidence and organizes it by the controls of a framework. Artifact gives AWS's own compliance reports, not evidence about the customer's environment.","Domain 6: Audit evidence",[null,"Artifact provides AWS's own compliance reports, not evidence about the customer's resources.","Inspector finds vulnerabilities; it does not collect audit evidence by control.","Trusted Advisor gives best practice recommendations, not framework-mapped evidence."],2],["x58",0,6,"A team runs its API on AWS Lambda. Under the shared responsibility model, which task belongs to the customer?",["Writing secure function code and limiting the function's IAM permissions","Patching the operating system and language runtime that run the functions","Maintaining the physical servers in the Region","Updating the hypervisor used by the Lambda fleet"],0,"For Lambda, AWS manages the infrastructure, operating system and runtime patching, while the customer is responsible for its code, configuration, data and IAM permissions. OS patching is the tempting choice, but that is AWS's job for Lambda.","Domain 6: Shared responsibility",[null,"Lambda's underlying operating system is patched by AWS, not the customer.","Physical servers and facilities are always AWS's responsibility.","Hypervisors and the virtualization layer are managed by AWS."],1],["x59",0,6,"A startup wants a free, structured review of one workload against AWS security best practices, with a list of high-risk issues and an improvement plan it can track. What should it use?",["AWS Shield Advanced","The AWS Well-Architected Tool with the Security Pillar questions","AWS Artifact agreements","Amazon Detective"],1,"The Well-Architected Tool guides a review against the pillars, including Security, and records high and medium risk issues with an improvement plan. Shield Advanced is a paid DDoS protection service, not a design review.","Domain 6: Security reviews",["Shield Advanced is paid DDoS protection and does not review workload design.",null,"Artifact agreements cover legal terms such as a business associate addendum, not workload reviews.","Detective investigates security findings; it does not assess architecture against best practices."],1],["x60",0,6,"The company has approved only two foundation models in Amazon Bedrock for business use. It wants to stop every member account from invoking any other model, whatever developers' IAM policies allow. What should it use?",["Amazon Bedrock Guardrails configured and attached to both of the approved models","Bedrock model invocation logging in each account","A CloudWatch alarm on Bedrock invocation counts","An SCP that denies bedrock:InvokeModel except for the approved model ARNs"],3,"An SCP with a Deny and a resource condition excluding the approved model ARNs caps what any principal in member accounts can invoke. Guardrails filter content of prompts and responses but do not restrict which models are called.","Domain 6: Generative AI governance",["Guardrails filter harmful or sensitive content; they do not prevent calls to other models.","Logging records invocations but does not stop unapproved models from being used.","An alarm on counts reacts after use and cannot block unapproved models.",null],2]]);
